window.OSData.searchIndex = [
  ["modules/01-fundamentals.html","Module 1: OS Fundamentals & System Calls","OS Fundamentals & System Calls","An operating system sits between applications and hardware. It shares the machine fairly, hides hardware details behind clean abstractions, and protects programs from one another.","Lesson"],
  ["modules/01-fundamentals.html#roles","Module 1: OS Fundamentals & System Calls","What an OS does","Resource manager: allocates CPU time, memory, storage and I/O devices among competing programs. Abstraction layer: turns messy hardware into files, processes, sockets and virtual memory. Protector: prevents one program (or user) from damaging another or the system. Control program: supervises execution to prevent errors and improper use of the computer. The kernel is the always-resident core that performs these jobs. Everything else (shells, compilers, browsers, even the GUI) is just a program that asks the kernel for help. The kernel does not start itself. When the machine powers on, firmware runs a boot loader, a small program that finds the kernel on disk, loads it into memory and starts it. Module 16 follows the whole boot, step by step.","Lesson"],
  ["modules/01-fundamentals.html#modes","Module 1: OS Fundamentals & System Calls","Kernel mode and user mode","The CPU has a mode bit. In user mode only a safe subset of instructions is allowed. In kernel mode every instruction and every memory address is available. Privileged instructions (changing page tables, disabling interrupts, talking to devices, halting the CPU) fault if tried in user mode. That hardware enforcement is what makes protection possible. Key idea: a program can never simply “jump into the kernel”. The only ways in are hardware-controlled entry points: interrupts, exceptions and traps (system calls).","Lesson"],
  ["modules/01-fundamentals.html#events","Module 1: OS Fundamentals & System Calls","Interrupts, traps and exceptions","EventSourceSynchronous?Example InterruptHardwareNoTimer tick, keyboard press, disk completed Exception (fault)CPU, the current instructionYesDivide by zero, page fault, illegal instruction TrapSoftware, deliberatelyYesSystem call instruction In every case the CPU saves enough state, switches to kernel mode, and jumps to a handler found in the interrupt vector. The timer interrupt is especially important: it lets the OS retake the CPU from a program that never gives it up.","Lesson"],
  ["modules/01-fundamentals.html#syscall","Module 1: OS Fundamentals & System Calls","System calls","A system call is the API through which programs request kernel services: files, processes, memory, networking. Programs normally call a library wrapper (like read() in libc) instead of issuing the trap themselves. Try different system calls. The kernel-side work differs, but the mode switch, argument validation and return path are always the same. That fixed cost is why batching I/O (bigger reads and writes) is faster than many tiny calls. Try it: a system call versus a library call Why C is the clearer version here: printf and write are exactly the two layers this lesson describes: a library function with a buffer, and a thin wrapper around the system call. Run it and you see the output order change, which is the buffer at work. Python behaves the same way, with different names. print() writes into a buffer owned by sys.stdout, and os.write() is the thin wrapper around the system call. The surprise is the same, but it is less visible in everyday Python because you rarely call os.write directly.","Lesson"],
  ["modules/01-fundamentals.html#structure","Module 1: OS Fundamentals & System Calls","OS structure","DesignIdeaProsConsExamples MonolithicAll services in one kernel address spaceFast: direct function callsA driver bug can crash everythingLinux, traditional UNIX MicrokernelMinimal kernel; other services run in user spaceIsolation, reliability, flexibilityMessage-passing overheadMinix, QNX, seL4 HybridMicrokernel ideas with many services in kernelCompromiseComplexWindows NT, macOS (XNU) Layered / modularStrict layers or loadable modulesEasier to reason about and extendLayering overheadLinux kernel modules Common confusion: the OS is not the shell, the GUI or the whole distribution. The kernel is the privileged core; the rest is user-space software. Beyond the big three: phones, cars, single-board computers and mainframes make very different choices about these same ideas. See Module 12 to compare Android, iOS, Qubes OS, Raspberry Pi OS, real-time systems and IBM's z/OS, z/VSE and IBM i. AdvancedGoing deeper: how a system call really enters the kernel The trap instruction. On x86-64 the wrapper executes syscall; on ARM64 it is svc. The CPU switches to kernel mode and jumps to one address chosen by the OS at boot, not by the program. The call number goes in a register, and the kernel uses it to index a table of handlers. Arguments are checked, never trusted. A pointer passed to the kernel could point at kernel memory. The kernel verifies that every user pointer lies in user space and copies data across the boundary (for example copy_from_user in Linux). The vDSO. Some calls, such as reading the clock, are so frequent that Linux avoids the mode switch: the kernel maps a small page of code and data into every process, and clock_gettime runs entirely in user mode. Monolithic versus micro kernels, in practice. A microkernel keeps drivers and file systems in user-mode servers, so one system call can become several messages. That costs speed but isolates faults. Hybrids such as Windows NT and macOS (XNU) put some of each in the kernel. Hardware support goes further than two modes. x86 has four privilege rings (only 0 and 3 are commonly used), and virtualization adds a mode below the kernel for the hypervisor. ARM calls these exception levels, EL0 to EL3.","Lesson"],
  ["modules/02-security.html","Module 2: Security & Access Control","Security & Access Control","Protection is the mechanism that controls who can do what to which resource. Security is the broader goal of defending against threats, including attackers that exploit weak protection.","Lesson"],
  ["modules/02-security.html#goals-and-principles","Module 2: Security & Access Control","Goals and principles","Confidentiality, integrity, availability (the CIA triad): keep secrets secret, keep data correct, keep services usable. Authentication answers “who are you?” (passwords, keys, biometrics, MFA). Authorization answers “what may you do?” (permissions, ACLs). Least privilege: give every user and program only the rights it needs, for only as long as it needs them. Defense in depth: several independent layers, so one failure does not mean compromise. Separation of policy and mechanism: the kernel provides the checking mechanism; administrators set the policy.","Lesson"],
  ["modules/02-security.html#unix-file-permissions","Module 2: Security & Access Control","Unix file permissions","Every file has an owner, a group, and three sets of read/write/execute bits: for the owner, the group and others. Each set is written as one octal digit (r=4, w=2, x=1). Special bits: setuid (run with the file owner's privileges), setgid, and the sticky bit (in a shared directory like /tmp, only a file's owner may delete it). Try it: umask and chmod Why C is the clearer version here: open, umask, chmod and stat are the real system calls, and the mode is an integer whose bits you can see. The octal constants (0666, 022) are the notation chmod uses too. Python’s os module mirrors the C calls almost one-to-one, so nothing here is contrived. The built-in open() has no mode argument, which is why the example uses os.open. On Windows, chmod can only toggle the read-only flag, so this example needs Linux or macOS.","Lesson"],
  ["modules/02-security.html#access-control-matrix","Module 2: Security & Access Control","Access-control matrix","Conceptually, protection is a matrix: rows are subjects (users, processes), columns are objects (files, devices), and each cell lists the permitted operations. Real systems never store the full sparse matrix. They store it by column (ACLs) or by row (capabilities).","Lesson"],
  ["modules/02-security.html#typical-attacks-and-defenses","Module 2: Security & Access Control","Typical attacks and defenses","ThreatWhat happensMitigation Buffer overflowInput larger than a buffer overwrites adjacent memory (e.g., a return address)Bounds checking, stack canaries, NX/DEP, ASLR, memory-safe languages Privilege escalationA flaw in a privileged (setuid/root) program gives an attacker more rightsLeast privilege, drop privileges early, patching, sandboxing MalwareViruses, worms, trojans, ransomwarePermissions, code signing, updates, backups, user training Password attacksGuessing, credential stuffing, stolen hashesSalted slow hashing, rate limiting, MFA Denial of serviceExhaust CPU, memory, disk or networkQuotas, rate limits, cgroups, redundancy Trade-off to discuss: stronger isolation (containers, sandboxes, mandatory access control like SELinux) costs convenience and sometimes performance. Security decisions are always about balancing risk, usability and cost. Practice the calculations: Unix permissions problems (random problems with worked solutions). AdvancedGoing deeper: beyond owner, group and others Access-control lists. Nine permission bits cannot say “Alice may read, Bob may write”. POSIX ACLs (getfacl, setfacl) and Windows ACLs attach a list of (principal, rights) entries to a file, which is the access-control matrix stored by column. Capabilities. Linux splits root’s power into about 40 capabilities such as CAP_NET_BIND_SERVICE, so a web server can bind port 80 without being able to read every file. This is least privilege applied to the kernel itself. Mandatory access control. With discretionary control the owner decides. SELinux, AppArmor and Android’s SELinux policy let the administrator define rules that even the owner (and root) cannot override. This is how a compromised app is confined. Setuid and its risks. A setuid program runs with its owner’s privileges, so passwd can edit the password file. Any bug in such a program becomes a privilege escalation, which is why the set of setuid binaries is kept small and audited. Address-space randomization and non-executable memory. ASLR places the stack, heap and libraries at random addresses, and NX/DEP forbids running data pages as code. Neither stops a bug, but together they make buffer-overflow exploits much harder to write.","Lesson"],
  ["modules/03-processes.html","Module 3: Processes","Processes","A program is a file on disk. A process is that program in execution, with its own address space, open files and CPU state.","Lesson"],
  ["modules/03-processes.html#what-a-process-contains","Module 3: Processes","What a process contains","Text (code), data (globals), heap (dynamic memory, grows up) and stack (function frames, grows down). CPU state: program counter, registers, stack pointer. Kernel bookkeeping in the process control block (PCB): PID, state, scheduling info, memory maps, open-file table, accounting.","Lesson"],
  ["modules/03-processes.html#process-states","Module 3: Processes","Process states","As a process runs, it moves between states. Only one process per CPU core can be Running; the rest are Ready (waiting in the ready queue for a CPU) or Waiting (blocked for I/O or an event). Notice: a process whose I/O completes goes to Ready, not Running. The scheduler alone decides who runs next. A process can never go Waiting → Running directly.","Lesson"],
  ["modules/03-processes.html#context-switching","Module 3: Processes","Context switching","A context switch saves the running process's registers into its PCB and loads another's. It is pure overhead (no user work happens), and it also disturbs caches and the TLB, so schedulers try not to switch needlessly. See Module 5 for how the cost trades off against responsiveness.","Lesson"],
  ["modules/03-processes.html#creating-processes-fork-exec-wait","Module 3: Processes","Creating processes: fork, exec, wait","fork() makes a copy of the calling process (using copy-on-write so it's cheap). The copy is the child and the original is its parent. It returns 0 in the child and the child's PID in the parent. exec() replaces the process image with a new program. wait() lets the parent collect the child's exit status. In Python the same primitives are available as os.fork(), os.execvp() and os.waitpid(), and higher-level as subprocess. How many processes? Every call to fork() doubles the number of processes, because both the parent and every existing child continue to execute the next line.","Lesson"],
  ["modules/03-processes.html#special-cases","Module 3: Processes","Special cases","Zombie: finished, but the parent hasn't called wait() yet, so the PCB lingers. Orphan: the parent exited first. The child is adopted by init/systemd (PID 1), which reaps it. Daemon: background service process with no controlling terminal.","Lesson"],
  ["modules/03-processes.html#interprocess-communication-ipc","Module 3: Processes","Interprocess communication (IPC)","Processes are isolated, so they cooperate through the kernel. Here is the short list; Module 13 explores each mechanism with a simulator and runnable C and Python examples. MechanismHow it worksGood for Shared memoryProcesses map the same physical pagesHigh-throughput data sharing (needs synchronization, see Module 6) Message passingsend / receive through the kernelSimpler, safer, works across machines PipesOne-way byte stream between processesShell pipelines (ls | grep txt) SocketsBidirectional, local or networkClient/server programs SignalsAsynchronous notificationsInterrupt, terminate, reload config Try it on a real computer: Lab 1: launching, waiting and forking processes. AdvancedGoing deeper: what fork really copies Copy-on-write. fork() does not copy the parent’s memory. The child gets copies of the page tables, with every writable page marked read-only. The first write by either process faults, and only then is that page copied. This is why fork followed by exec is cheap. vfork, posix_spawn and clone. When the child will immediately call exec, even copying page tables is wasted. posix_spawn creates the new program directly, and Linux’s clone lets the caller choose what is shared, which is how threads and containers are built. Windows has no fork. CreateProcess builds a new process running a named program in one step, and the family of calls on this page is a Unix design choice rather than a law of operating systems. Zombies and orphans. A child that exits stays in the process table as a zombie until the parent calls wait, because the exit status must be kept. If the parent dies first, the child is adopted by init (PID 1), which reaps it. A long-running parent that never waits slowly fills the table. Pipes are just file descriptors. A pipe is a kernel buffer with two descriptors, and a shell builds a | b by forking twice, connecting standard output to one end and standard input to the other, then calling exec. The programs never know.","Lesson"],
  ["modules/04-scheduling.html","Module 4: CPU Scheduling","CPU Scheduling","With more runnable processes than CPU cores, the scheduler decides who runs, when and for how long. Different goals lead to different algorithms. A process alternates between computing and waiting for I/O. One continuous run on the CPU is a CPU burst, and a process that mostly waits for devices is I/O-bound. The scheduler chooses the next process and the dispatcher hands it the CPU (performing the context switch). Preemption means the OS can take the CPU away from a running process before its burst is over.","Lesson"],
  ["modules/04-scheduling.html#scheduling-criteria","Module 4: CPU Scheduling","Scheduling criteria","MetricDefinitionWe want it… CPU utilizationFraction of time the CPU is busyhigh ThroughputProcesses completed per unit timehigh Turnaround timecompletion − arrivallow Waiting timetime spent in the ready queue = turnaround − burstlow Response timefirst run − arrivallow (important for interactive systems)","Lesson"],
  ["modules/04-scheduling.html#the-algorithms","Module 4: CPU Scheduling","The algorithms","AlgorithmPreemptive?IdeaWatch out for FCFSNoRun in arrival orderConvoy effect SJFNoRun the shortest next burstNeeds burst prediction; starves long jobs SRTFYesPreemptive SJF: run the least remaining timeMore context switches; starvation Round RobinYesEach process gets a quantum in turnQuantum too small means overhead; too large means FCFS PriorityEitherHighest priority firstStarvation, fixed with aging","Lesson"],
  ["modules/04-scheduling.html#simulator","Module 4: CPU Scheduling","Simulator","Load a preset or edit the process table. For example, load Convoy effect and compare FCFS to SJF and Round Robin, then try different quanta.","Lesson"],
  ["modules/04-scheduling.html#things-to-try","Module 4: CPU Scheduling","Things to try","On the Textbook preset, switch FCFS → SJF. Average waiting time drops from 6.67 to 4.00. SJF is provably optimal for average waiting time when bursts are known. On Round Robin example, slide the quantum from 1 to 12. What happens to context switches? To response time? Where does RR start to look like FCFS? Add a long process arriving at time 0 and several short ones arriving at time 1. Which algorithms suffer the most? On Staggered arrivals, compare SJF with SRTF. When does preemption help?","Lesson"],
  ["modules/04-scheduling.html#beyond-the-textbook","Module 4: CPU Scheduling","Beyond the textbook","Module 17 simulates all of these: feedback queues, real-time guarantees and multicore balancing. Multilevel feedback queues (MLFQ) approximate SJF without knowing burst lengths: new jobs start at high priority and sink if they use their whole quantum. Linux uses a fair scheduler (CFS, and more recently EEVDF) that tracks how much CPU time each task has received and picks the one furthest behind. Real-time systems use algorithms such as rate-monotonic or earliest-deadline-first, where meeting deadlines matters more than average speed. Multicore schedulers add load balancing and processor affinity, because migrating a task throws away its warm caches. Practice the calculations: CPU scheduling problems (random problems with worked solutions). Try it on a real computer: Lab 3: reading CPU use and process states with psutil. AdvancedGoing deeper: how real schedulers work Multilevel feedback queues. Real systems cannot know a job’s length in advance, so they estimate it from behavior: a task that uses its whole time slice drops to a lower-priority queue, and one that blocks early stays high. Interactive programs are favored without anyone labeling them. Linux CFS and EEVDF. The Completely Fair Scheduler tracks each task’s virtual runtime and always runs the one that has had the least, kept in a red-black tree. Newer kernels replace it with EEVDF, which also considers each task’s latency requirement. Multicore adds a second problem. Each core usually has its own run queue, so the OS must balance load without losing cache affinity: a task moved to another core starts with a cold cache. Processor affinity lets a program pin itself to specific cores. Real-time scheduling. Rate-monotonic scheduling gives the highest priority to the shortest period, and earliest-deadline-first always runs the task whose deadline is nearest. Both have schedulability tests, and a missed deadline is a failure, not a slowdown. Priority inversion. If a low-priority task holds a lock that a high-priority task needs, a medium-priority task can run ahead of both. The 1997 Mars Pathfinder resets were caused by exactly this. The fix is priority inheritance: the lock holder temporarily takes the waiter’s priority.","Lesson"],
  ["modules/05-threads.html","Module 5: Threads & Parallel Performance","Threads & Parallel Performance","A thread is a unit of CPU execution inside a process. Threads share the process's memory, so they can cooperate cheaply, but that sharing is also where the danger lies.","Lesson"],
  ["modules/05-threads.html#threads-vs-processes","Module 5: Threads & Parallel Performance","Threads vs. processes","PropertyProcessThread (within a process) Address spaceOwnShared with the other threads Heap, globals, open filesOwnShared Stack, registers, program counterOwnOwn (per thread) Creation / switch costHigherLower (no address-space switch) IsolationStrong: a crash stays in one processWeak: one bad thread can corrupt or crash all Benefits: responsiveness (UI stays alive while work runs), resource sharing, cheaper creation, and scalability across cores.","Lesson"],
  ["modules/05-threads.html#threading-models","Module 5: Threads & Parallel Performance","Threading models","User-level threads are managed by a library. They are fast, but the kernel sees one thread, so a blocking system call stalls all of them (many-to-one). Kernel-level threads are scheduled by the OS. A blocking call only blocks one thread and threads can use several cores (one-to-one, used by Linux, Windows and macOS). Thread pools reuse a fixed set of worker threads, avoiding creation cost and bounding resource use. Try it: split work across threads Why C is the clearer version here: POSIX threads give true parallelism. The four threads can run on four cores at the same time, and the program is a direct picture of the lesson: split the work, start the threads, join them, combine the results. The structure is the same, but speed is not. In standard CPython a global interpreter lock lets only one thread run Python code at a time, so this pure-Python sum is no faster with four threads than with one. Threads still help when they wait for I/O. For CPU-bound work Python programs use multiprocessing or a free-threaded build. The example shows the pattern, not the speedup.","Lesson"],
  ["modules/05-threads.html#how-much-speedup-can-you-expect","Module 5: Threads & Parallel Performance","How much speedup can you expect?","Amdahl's law says that if a fraction p of a program can be parallelized across N cores, then speedup = 1 / ((1 − p) + p / N) The serial fraction (1 − p) sets a hard ceiling of 1/(1 − p) no matter how many cores you add. Gustafson's law gives the more optimistic view that when you have more cores you usually also solve bigger problems. Try it: set p = 90% and add cores. The curve flattens near 10×. Going from 8 to 16 cores buys little, so work on the serial part instead.","Lesson"],
  ["modules/05-threads.html#the-price-of-switching","Module 5: Threads & Parallel Performance","The price of switching","Each context switch costs time without doing useful work. If the quantum is q and a switch costs s, the useful fraction is q / (q + s). But a longer quantum makes every process wait longer between turns. Practice the calculations: Amdahl's law problems (random problems with worked solutions). Try it on a real computer: Lab 2: threads, races and the GIL. AdvancedGoing deeper: what threads cost and what they buy Thread stacks and thread-local storage. Each thread has its own stack (typically 8 MiB of address space on Linux, committed lazily) and its own thread-local variables, which is why a thousand threads uses far more address space than a thousand function calls. Many-to-many models and user-level threads. Go goroutines and Erlang processes are scheduled by the language runtime on top of a few kernel threads, so creating one costs a few kilobytes and a switch costs no system call. The price is that a blocking system call must be handled by the runtime. Event loops as an alternative. A server can handle thousands of connections on one thread by waiting with epoll, kqueue or io_uring and never blocking. Node.js and Python’s asyncio are built this way. Amdahl versus Gustafson. Amdahl’s law fixes the problem size. Gustafson’s observation is that larger machines are used for larger problems, so the serial fraction matters less than the formula suggests when the parallel part grows with the machine. False sharing. Two threads writing different variables that happen to sit in the same 64-byte cache line force that line to bounce between cores. Padding the variables apart can make a program several times faster without changing its logic.","Lesson"],
  ["modules/06-synchronization.html","Module 6: Process Synchronization","Process Synchronization","When concurrent threads or processes share data, the result can depend on exact timing. Synchronization tools enforce order and exclusive access.","Lesson"],
  ["modules/06-synchronization.html#the-race-condition","Module 6: Process Synchronization","The race condition","A race condition exists when the outcome depends on how operations interleave. Even counter++ is three machine steps (load, add, store), so two threads can both load the same old value and one increment disappears. Step the threads by hand: run thread 1's LOAD, then thread 2's LOAD, then everything else. Then switch on the mutex and notice that thread 2 is forced to wait.","Lesson"],
  ["modules/06-synchronization.html#the-critical-section-problem","Module 6: Process Synchronization","The critical-section problem","The code that touches shared data is the critical section. A correct solution must provide: Mutual exclusion: only one thread inside at a time. Progress: if no one is inside, a thread that wants in is not delayed forever by threads that don't. Bounded waiting: a limit on how often others can overtake a waiting thread.","Lesson"],
  ["modules/06-synchronization.html#tools","Module 6: Process Synchronization","Tools","ToolHow it worksNotes Atomic instructionsHardware test-and-set / compare-and-swapFoundation for all other locks MutexLock with an owner; waiters sleepBest for longer critical sections SpinlockWaiter busy-loops until the lock is freeGood for very short sections on multicore; wasteful otherwise SemaphoreInteger with atomic wait() (P) and signal() (V)Binary (mutex-like) or counting (resource pools) Monitor / condition variableLanguage-level lock plus wait/notify queuesJava synchronized, Python threading.Condition. See Module 15 Try it: a spinlock built from test-and-set Why C is the clearer version here: atomic_flag_test_and_set is the hardware instruction the lesson talks about: it sets the flag and returns the old value in one indivisible step. Spinning on it is a real spinlock. (This example needs C11 for <stdatomic.h>.) This is more contrived in Python. Pure Python has no atomic test-and-set, so a threading.Lock stands in for the flag, and the loop yields the CPU with time.sleep(0) because a bare busy loop is very slow under the global interpreter lock. Python programs almost never spin; they just call with lock: and let the OS put the waiter to sleep. Read the example for the idea, not as a pattern to copy.","Lesson"],
  ["modules/06-synchronization.html#bounded-buffer-producer-consumer","Module 6: Process Synchronization","Bounded buffer (producer–consumer)","Producers add items to a buffer of N slots and consumers remove them. Three semaphores solve it: empty = N (free slots), full = 0 (filled slots) and mutex = 1 (protects the buffer structure). Order matters: if a producer does wait(mutex) before wait(empty) and the buffer is full, it sleeps while holding the mutex, so the consumer can never get in to make room. That is a deadlock. See Module 7.","Lesson"],
  ["modules/06-synchronization.html#other-classic-problems","Module 6: Process Synchronization","Other classic problems","Readers–writers: many readers may share data, but writers need exclusive access (beware writer starvation). Module 15 simulates the policies. Dining philosophers: see Module 7. Priority inversion: a high-priority task blocked on a lock held by a low-priority task that is preempted by a medium one. Fix with priority inheritance. Try it on a real computer: Lab 2: a race condition and a lock · Lab 4: semaphores and a bounded buffer. AdvancedGoing deeper: lock-free ideas and what hardware really guarantees Compare-and-swap. The instruction at the heart of lock-free code atomically sets a location to a new value only if it still holds the expected old one. A thread retries in a loop until it succeeds, so no thread can block another by sleeping inside a critical section. Memory ordering. Compilers and CPUs reorder reads and writes. On x86 stores are seen in order but a store may be delayed past a later load, and ARM allows far more reordering. Atomic operations carry an ordering (acquire, release, sequentially consistent) that tells both what they may not move. Why Peterson’s algorithm fails on modern hardware. It is correct only if each thread’s writes become visible to the other in program order. Without memory barriers, real CPUs break that assumption, so the textbook proof does not apply to real machines unless the algorithm uses atomics. Futexes. Linux mutexes are fast because the uncontended case is one atomic instruction in user mode. Only when a thread must wait does it call the kernel (futex) to sleep, and the unlocker wakes it. So the system call cost is paid only under contention. The ABA problem. A compare-and-swap can succeed even though the value changed from A to B and back to A in between, which corrupts lock-free stacks that reuse memory. Version counters or hazard pointers address it. Read-copy-update and transactional memory. RCU lets readers proceed with no locks at all while a writer publishes a new copy, which is why the Linux kernel uses it for read-mostly data. Hardware transactional memory tried to make whole critical sections speculative, but it has had limited success.","Lesson"],
  ["modules/07-deadlocks.html","Module 7: Deadlocks","Deadlocks","A deadlock is a set of processes that are each waiting for a resource held by another in the set, so none of them will ever proceed.","Lesson"],
  ["modules/07-deadlocks.html#four-necessary-conditions-coffman","Module 7: Deadlocks","Four necessary conditions (Coffman)","All four must hold at the same time for deadlock to occur. Break any one and deadlock is impossible. ConditionMeaningHow to break it Mutual exclusionA resource can be used by only one process at a timeMake resources sharable (read-only files, lock-free structures). Not always possible. Hold and waitA process holds some resources while waiting for othersRequest everything at once, or release before requesting more No preemptionResources can only be released voluntarilyAllow the OS to take resources back (works for CPU/memory, not for printers or locks) Circular waitA cycle P1→P2→…→P1 of waitingImpose a global ordering on resources and always acquire in that order A resource-allocation graph shows these relationships: an edge from a process to a resource means “requests”; from a resource to a process means “held by”. With one instance of each resource, a cycle means deadlock. With multiple instances, a cycle is necessary but not sufficient.","Lesson"],
  ["modules/07-deadlocks.html#philosophers","Module 7: Deadlocks","Dining philosophers","Five philosophers sit around a table with one fork between each pair. A philosopher needs both adjacent forks to eat. This small puzzle contains all four deadlock conditions. Run the naive strategy a few times with different seeds. If every philosopher grabs their left fork at the same moment, nobody can ever eat. Then compare the fixes. Resource ordering removes circular wait: one philosopher picks up forks in the opposite order from the others. Limiting the table to N−1 philosophers guarantees at least one can get both forks. Asymmetric pickup order (odd vs. even) also breaks the cycle.","Lesson"],
  ["modules/07-deadlocks.html#handling-deadlock-four-strategies","Module 7: Deadlocks","Handling deadlock: four strategies","StrategyIdeaCost PreventionDesign so that one of the four conditions can never holdMay reduce resource utilization or concurrency AvoidanceGrant a request only if the system stays in a safe stateNeeds advance knowledge of maximum demands Detection & recoveryLet it happen, find the cycle, kill or roll back a victimDetection overhead; lost work Ignore it (“ostrich”)Assume it's rare; let users rebootUsed by many general-purpose OSes","Lesson"],
  ["modules/07-deadlocks.html#banker-s-algorithm","Module 7: Deadlocks","Banker's algorithm","The Banker's algorithm keeps matrices Allocation, Max and Available, and derives Need = Max − Allocation. A state is safe if some ordering lets every process obtain its Need, finish, and release its resources. To check a request, pretend to grant it and test the safety of the resulting state. Try it: in the safe preset, ask P4 for (3, 3, 0). There are enough available resources, but granting it leaves no process able to finish, so the Banker refuses. Then switch to the unsafe preset to see the safety search fail. Safe ⇒ no deadlock. Unsafe does not mean deadlock has happened, just that it can no longer be ruled out. Practice the calculations: Banker's algorithm problems (random problems with worked solutions). Try it on a real computer: Lab 4: create a real deadlock and prevent it. AdvancedGoing deeper: deadlock in the real world Lock ordering in practice. Large programs avoid deadlock by assigning every lock a rank and always acquiring in increasing order. The Linux kernel’s lockdep checker watches every lock acquisition at run time and warns about an ordering cycle even if the deadlock never actually happened. Livelock and starvation. In a livelock the threads are not blocked but keep reacting to one another (two people stepping aside in a corridor), so no progress is made. Starvation is a thread that never gets a turn even though the system is moving. Neither is a deadlock, and neither is fixed by the same remedies. Distributed and database deadlock. Databases detect a deadlock by finding a cycle in the wait-for graph, choose a victim transaction, roll it back and let the application retry. Two-phase locking makes deadlock possible, and timeouts are the fallback when no coordinator exists. Why operating systems mostly ignore it. Prevention restricts programs and avoidance needs advance knowledge of every maximum claim. Most general-purpose systems take the ostrich approach for application resources and rely on programmers to order locks, because deadlocks are rare and the cost of the alternatives is paid constantly. Priority inversion looks like deadlock. A task blocked behind a lock holder that cannot run appears hung. It is solved by priority inheritance rather than by any of the four strategies on this page.","Lesson"],
  ["modules/08-memory.html","Module 8: Memory Management","Memory Management","Programs must be in memory to run. The OS decides where, keeps processes out of each other's memory, and makes limited RAM feel large and private.","Lesson"],
  ["modules/08-memory.html#logical-vs-physical-addresses","Module 8: Memory Management","Logical vs. physical addresses","The CPU generates logical (virtual) addresses. The MMU translates each one into a physical address at run time. That indirection lets the OS relocate programs, enforce protection and share memory without the program noticing.","Lesson"],
  ["modules/08-memory.html#contiguous-allocation","Module 8: Memory Management","Contiguous allocation","The simplest scheme gives each process one contiguous block. When a request arrives, the OS must pick a free hole: First-fit: first hole that is big enough (fast). Best-fit: smallest hole that fits (leaves tiny leftover slivers). Worst-fit: largest hole (leaves big leftovers, but destroys large holes). Over time, free memory shatters into many small holes: external fragmentation. Compaction fixes it by sliding processes together, at the cost of copying and relocating them. Try it: click “Load fragmented example”, then request 150 KB. There are 330 KB free in total but the largest hole is only 100 KB. Try compaction. Then reset and run the same sequence under best-fit and worst-fit.","Lesson"],
  ["modules/08-memory.html#paging","Module 8: Memory Management","Paging","Paging splits the logical address space into fixed-size pages and physical memory into same-size frames. A page table maps each page to a frame, so a process's memory need not be contiguous. This removes external fragmentation; the cost is internal fragmentation in each process's last page (about half a page on average). A logical address splits into two parts: page number (high bits, index into the page table) and offset (low bits, position inside the page). For a page size of 2n bytes, the offset is n bits. For example, a 32-bit address with 4 KiB pages has a 20-bit page number and 12-bit offset.","Lesson"],
  ["modules/08-memory.html#multilevel","Module 8: Memory Management","Why one big page table does not work","Take a 32-bit machine with 4 KiB pages. The page number has 20 bits, so a flat page table needs 220 = 1,048,576 entries. At 4 bytes each, that is 4 MiB for every process, even a tiny one that uses a few pages. A hundred processes would spend 400 MiB just on tables. With 64-bit addresses a flat table is impossible: a 48-bit address space would need 236 entries, which is 512 GiB per process. Notice what is wasteful: most of a process's address space is empty. A typical process has a little code at the bottom, a heap above it, some libraries, and a stack at the very top, with huge gaps between them. We should not pay for table entries that describe nothing.","Lesson"],
  ["modules/08-memory.html#multi-level-page-tables","Module 8: Memory Management","Multi-level page tables","A multi-level page table applies the paging idea to the page table itself. The page number is cut into pieces. The first piece indexes a small top-level table. Each entry points to a second-level table, and the second piece indexes that. Second-level tables are created only for regions the process actually uses. If a whole region is empty, its top-level entry is simply marked not present and no lower table exists. The classic 32-bit x86 layout is 10 + 10 + 12 bits: 10 bits for the top-level index, 10 for the second-level index, and 12 for the offset within a 4 KiB page. Each table has 1,024 entries of 4 bytes, which is exactly one page, so the tables themselves can be stored in ordinary page-sized frames. For the address 0x00403004 the top-level index is 1, the second-level index is 3 and the offset is 4. A typical small process needs one top-level table and about three second-level tables: 16 KiB instead of 4 MiB. On 64-bit x86 the layout is 9 + 9 + 9 + 9 + 12 bits (48-bit addresses): four levels of 512 entries, each table exactly one 4 KiB page of 8-byte entries. Try it: follow a translation Pick an address layout and a TLB size, then step through the examples. Watch which table is read at each level, what happens when an entry is missing (a page fault), and how much memory the tables use compared with one flat table. Try “Cycle through six pages” with a 4-entry TLB, then with 8 entries. The same idea in code This tiny machine uses 16-bit addresses (4 + 4 + 8 bits) so every table fits on screen. Both versions print identical output, and the numbers in the comments line up in both tabs: (1) a second-level table is created only when needed; (2)–(5) split the address with shifts and masks; (6) a missing table means a page fault; (7) the frame number is placed in front of the unchanged offset. Why C is the clearer version here: a page table is a block of fixed-size entries in memory that the hardware reads directly. C shows that honestly: arrays of fixed-size structs, pointers for the next level, and explicit bit masks. A real entry is a packed machine word with flag bits next to the frame number; here each entry is just a frame number to keep the idea visible. Python is fine for the logic, but it hides the hardware reality. Lists and None play the roles of arrays and the “not present” marker, and Python decides how big each entry is, so it is a little contrived as a model of a real page table. The shifts, masks and the two-step lookup are exactly the same, ","Lesson"],
  ["modules/08-memory.html#what-is-in-a-page-table-entry","Module 8: Memory Management","What is in a page-table entry?","Each entry holds the frame number and a few flag bits that the hardware checks on every access. A page-table entry (PTE) on x86 includes: FlagMeaningUsed for PresentThe page is in a frame right nowDemand paging: a clear bit raises a page fault Read/writeWrites are allowedProtecting code and shared data; copy-on-write starts read-only User/supervisorUser-mode code may access itKeeping the kernel's memory away from programs (kernel and user mode) AccessedThe page was read or written recentlyThe reference bit for page replacement (Clock) DirtyThe page was written since it was loadedKnowing whether it must be saved before eviction No-executeInstructions may not run from this pageStopping injected data from running as code (security)","Lesson"],
  ["modules/08-memory.html#making-translation-fast-the-tlb","Module 8: Memory Management","Making translation fast: the TLB","A multi-level table saves memory but makes a translation slower: every level is another memory read. Reading a table in memory in order to translate an address is called a page walk. With four levels, one memory access could become five. Programs would crawl, were it not for the TLB, a small hardware cache of recent translations. A hit skips the walk completely. A miss walks the tables and then caches the result. With TLB hit ratio h, n table levels, TLB lookup time tTLB and memory time tmem: EAT = h × (tTLB + tmem) + (1 − h) × (tTLB + n × tmem + tmem) With a 1 ns TLB, 100 ns memory and a 98% hit ratio, a single-level table gives EAT = 0.98 × 101 + 0.02 × 201 = 103 ns. With two levels it is 105 ns, and with four levels 109 ns. Even with deep tables the cost stays under 10%, as long as the hit ratio is high. It is high because programs show locality of reference: they keep reusing a small set of pages. The TLB reach (entries × page size) is how much memory the TLB can cover at once: 64 entries of 4 KiB cover only 256 KiB. AdvancedGoing deeper: how real hardware does it Page-walk caches. CPUs also cache the upper-level entries, so a TLB miss often needs only one or two memory reads, not n. The EAT formula above is the worst case. Address-space identifiers. Each process has its own page tables, so after a context switch the old TLB entries are wrong. Without help the OS must flush the TLB on every switch, so the new process starts with a cold TLB. An ASID (x86 calls it PCID) tags each entry with its process, so entries from several processes can live together and no flush is needed. Huge pages. A huge page of 2 MiB (or 1 GiB) skips the last table level, because the offset grows to 21 bits (or 30). One TLB entry then maps 512× (or 262,144×) more memory, which greatly extends the TLB reach for databases and virtual machines. The cost is internal fragmentation and needing a large contiguous run of frames. Linux can create them automatically (“transparent huge pages”). More levels. Newer x86 CPUs support five levels (57-bit addresses) for machines with more than 256 TiB of address space. RISC-V defines Sv39, Sv48 and Sv57 (3, 4 and 5 levels); ARM supports 4 KiB, 16 KiB and 64 KiB page sizes with different level splits. More levels mean longer walks. Virtual machines pay twice. A guest OS has its own page tables, and the hypervisor translates guest “physical” addresses again (nested or extended page tables). With 4 levels each, one TLB miss can need up to (4 + 1) ","Lesson"],
  ["modules/08-memory.html#other-designs","Module 8: Memory Management","Other designs","Hashed and inverted page tables have one entry per physical frame (or hash bucket) instead of per virtual page, trading lookup time for table size. They suit very large address spaces. Segmentation divides memory by logical units (code, stack, heap). Modern systems mostly use paging, sometimes with segment-like protection. Practice the calculations: address translation problems · memory allocation problems (random problems with worked solutions). Try it on a real computer: Lab 3: reading memory use with psutil.","Lesson"],
  ["modules/09-virtual-memory.html","Module 9: Virtual Memory","Virtual Memory","Virtual memory separates what a program thinks it has (a huge private address space) from the RAM that actually exists. Only the pages in active use need to be resident.","Lesson"],
  ["modules/09-virtual-memory.html#demand-paging","Module 9: Virtual Memory","Demand paging","With demand paging a page is loaded only when first referenced. Each page-table entry has a valid bit. When the CPU touches an invalid page, a page fault traps to the OS: Check that the reference is legal (otherwise: segmentation fault). Find a free frame. If none, choose a victim with a replacement algorithm (writing it out first if its dirty bit is set). Schedule a disk read for the needed page; the process blocks meanwhile. Update the page table and TLB, then restart the faulting instruction. This works because of locality of reference: programs reuse nearby and recently used memory, so faults are rare. Try it: watch pages being assigned Why C is the clearer version here: mmap reserves addresses and getrusage reports how many minor page faults this process has taken, so the program shows demand paging directly. The numbers are not printed, because they differ between systems; the program prints only the comparison the lesson is about. Python has the same two calls (mmap and resource.getrusage), so it is not contrived, but it is the only way to see this in Python. An ordinary Python list lives inside memory the interpreter manages, so you cannot tell when the kernel assigned each page. Needs Linux or macOS.","Lesson"],
  ["modules/09-virtual-memory.html#page-replacement-algorithms","Module 9: Virtual Memory","Page-replacement algorithms","AlgorithmEvictsNotes FIFOThe oldest page in memorySimple; suffers Belady's anomaly OPTThe page not needed for the longest time in the futureOptimal, but needs the future, so it is a yardstick LRUThe page least recently usedGood approximation of OPT; costly to track exactly Clock (second chance)First page found with reference bit 0, clearing bits as the hand sweepsCheap LRU approximation used in practice Experiments: (1) Use the Belady preset with FIFO and watch the faults-vs-frames chart rise from 3 to 4 frames. (2) Switch to LRU and OPT: they never get worse with more frames. (3) Use the Sequential scan preset with LRU and 4 frames. Why does LRU do terribly on a loop slightly larger than memory?","Lesson"],
  ["modules/09-virtual-memory.html#effective-access-time","Module 9: Virtual Memory","Effective access time","If memory takes tm and a page fault costs tf (milliseconds for a disk, so about 100,000× slower), then EAT = (1 − p) × tm + p × tf. A fault rate of 1 in 1000 on a 100 ns memory with an 8 ms fault already slows the system 81×.","Lesson"],
  ["modules/09-virtual-memory.html#thrashing-and-the-working-set","Module 9: Virtual Memory","Thrashing and the working set","If a process doesn't have enough frames for its current locality, it faults constantly, and the CPU sits idle waiting for the disk. The OS then (wrongly) adds more processes and makes it worse: thrashing. The cure is the working-set model (give each process the pages it has used in the last Δ references) or page-fault-frequency control, combined with suspending some processes when memory is overcommitted.","Lesson"],
  ["modules/09-virtual-memory.html#other-virtual-memory-ideas","Module 9: Virtual Memory","Other virtual-memory ideas","Swapping moves whole processes (or, in modern systems, individual pages) between RAM and the backing store on disk. Copy-on-write makes fork() cheap by sharing pages until someone writes. Memory-mapped files let you access file data through ordinary loads and stores. Prepaging, huge pages and NUMA policies are performance refinements on the same foundation. Practice the calculations: page replacement problems · effective access time problems (random problems with worked solutions). Try it on a real computer: Lab 3: RAM, swap and process memory (RSS). AdvancedGoing deeper: what the kernel does on a page fault Three kinds of fault. A minor fault maps a page that is already in memory (a zero page, or a page cached by another process). A major fault must read from disk. An invalid fault touches an address that is not mapped at all and ends in SIGSEGV. Approximating LRU. True LRU would need a timestamp on every memory access. Hardware instead sets an accessed bit in the page-table entry, and the OS scans pages with the clock algorithm. Linux keeps active and inactive lists, and a page must be touched while inactive to earn promotion. Memory-mapped files and the page cache. read() usually copies from the kernel’s page cache, and mmap maps those same pages into the process. File data and program memory compete for the same frames, which is why free memory on a busy Linux machine looks low yet is mostly reclaimable cache. Swap, zswap and the OOM killer. When memory is short, anonymous pages go to swap (or are compressed in RAM with zswap). If reclaim fails, Linux’s out-of-memory killer picks a process to terminate. Mobile systems skip swap and kill background apps instead. Overcommit. Linux will happily promise more memory than exists, relying on the fact that most pages are never touched. The page-fault example above is why this works, and also why a program can be killed long after malloc said yes. Prefetching and Belady’s anomaly. The OS reads ahead when it sees sequential access. And FIFO can produce more faults with more frames (Belady’s anomaly), while stack algorithms such as LRU cannot.","Lesson"],
  ["modules/10-disk-io.html","Module 10: Disk Scheduling & I/O","Disk Scheduling & I/O","Devices are slow, diverse and unpredictable. The OS hides their differences behind uniform interfaces and arranges requests so the hardware is used efficiently.","Lesson"],
  ["modules/10-disk-io.html#talking-to-devices","Module 10: Disk Scheduling & I/O","Talking to devices","TechniqueHow it worksTrade-off PollingCPU repeatedly checks a device's status registerSimple, low latency for fast devices; wastes CPU on slow ones InterruptsDevice signals the CPU when readyCPU does useful work meanwhile; each interrupt has overhead DMAA controller moves whole blocks between device and memoryCPU involved only at start and finish; ideal for bulk transfers Memory-mapped I/ODevice registers appear as memory addressesUniform load/store programming model The kernel's I/O subsystem adds buffering (smoothing speed differences), caching (keeping hot data in RAM), spooling (queueing output for exclusive devices such as printers) and device drivers that translate generic requests into device-specific commands. With asynchronous I/O a program starts a request and keeps running, and the OS reports completion later (usually through an interrupt or callback). With synchronous (blocking) I/O the process waits until the request is done.","Lesson"],
  ["modules/10-disk-io.html#how-a-hard-disk-spends-its-time","Module 10: Disk Scheduling & I/O","How a hard disk spends its time","Access time = seek + rotational latency + transfer. Seek time (moving the head to the right cylinder) is by far the largest term, a few milliseconds. Rotational latency averages half a revolution (about 4.2 ms at 7200 RPM). Transfer of a block is tiny by comparison. So the OS reorders requests to minimize head movement.","Lesson"],
  ["modules/10-disk-io.html#disk-scheduling-algorithms","Module 10: Disk Scheduling & I/O","Disk-scheduling algorithms","AlgorithmRuleStrength / weakness FCFSServe in arrival orderFair, but lots of back-and-forth movement SSTFNearest request nextLow movement; can starve distant requests SCAN (elevator)Sweep to one end, then reverseNo starvation; favors the middle cylinders C-SCANSweep one way; jump back and sweep againMore uniform waiting time LOOK / C-LOOKLike SCAN/C-SCAN but turn around at the last request, not the disk edgeSlightly less movement Try it: load the textbook queue (head at 53) and confirm FCFS = 640 and SSTF = 236 cylinders. Then use Random queue several times. Does SSTF always beat FCFS? Is SCAN ever worse than SSTF? Increase the queue near the head and imagine a request at cylinder 199 waiting. That is the starvation risk.","Lesson"],
  ["modules/10-disk-io.html#beyond-spinning-disks","Module 10: Disk Scheduling & I/O","Beyond spinning disks","SSDs have no seek time. Their schedulers care about parallelism across flash channels, and the controller handles wear leveling, garbage collection and write amplification. Simple “none” or deadline schedulers are common. RAID combines disks for speed or reliability: RAID 0 stripes (fast, no redundancy), RAID 1 mirrors, RAID 5 stripes with distributed parity (survives one failure), RAID 6 survives two. Block layer merges adjacent requests and reorders them before they reach the device. Linux's mq-deadline, bfq and none are examples. Practice the calculations: disk scheduling problems (random problems with worked solutions). Try it on a real computer: Lab 3: disk usage and I/O counters. AdvancedGoing deeper: how storage really behaves Command queues. SATA drives accept up to 32 outstanding requests (NCQ) and reorder them in firmware, and NVMe supports up to 65,535 queues of 65,536 commands. The disk-scheduling algorithms on this page matter less when the device itself knows the geometry better than the OS does. The I/O scheduler on flash. With no head to move, Linux uses the none or mq-deadline scheduler on SSDs and NVMe devices. SCAN and C-SCAN remain useful mainly for spinning disks. Write amplification and TRIM. Flash cannot overwrite a page; it must erase a whole block first. The SSD’s translation layer remaps writes and later garbage-collects, which is why writing a byte can cost far more than a byte internally, and why the OS tells the drive which blocks are free (TRIM). RAID and what it does not do. Striping gives speed, mirroring gives redundancy, and parity (RAID 5/6) trades capacity for tolerance. RAID is not a backup: it protects against a failed disk, not against deletion, corruption or fire. DMA and the IOMMU. A device transfers data directly to memory without the CPU copying it. An IOMMU gives each device its own address translation, so a buggy or malicious device cannot read arbitrary memory. This is the page-table idea applied to hardware. Polling returns. At millions of operations per second, interrupts themselves become a cost, so io_uring and user-space NVMe drivers poll queues instead. The textbook trade-off between polling and interrupts reverses as devices get faster.","Lesson"],
  ["modules/11-file-systems.html","Module 11: File Systems","File Systems","A file system turns a raw array of disk blocks into named, persistent, protected files and directories.","Lesson"],
  ["modules/11-file-systems.html#files-and-directories","Module 11: File Systems","Files and directories","A file is a named collection of bytes plus metadata: size, owner, permissions, timestamps, location of its blocks. A directory is itself a file that maps names to files (in Unix: names to inode numbers). Programs refer to open files via a file descriptor, an index into a per-process table that points to system-wide open-file entries. Access methods: sequential (read next), direct/random (read block k) and indexed.","Lesson"],
  ["modules/11-file-systems.html#allocating-blocks-to-files","Module 11: File Systems","Allocating blocks to files","How a file's blocks are placed on disk determines speed, space efficiency and how well files can grow. Contiguous: fast and simple, but suffers external fragmentation and growth problems (like contiguous memory allocation). Linked: no external fragmentation; terrible random access, and one lost pointer loses the rest of the file. FAT: moves the links into a table (the File Allocation Table), usually cached in memory; simple and robust but the table can grow large. Indexed / inode-based: an index structure lists the blocks; good random access with no external fragmentation.","Lesson"],
  ["modules/11-file-systems.html#inodes","Module 11: File Systems","Inodes","An inode stores everything about a file except its name: permissions, owner, timestamps, size and block pointers. A classic Unix inode has about 12 direct pointers (fast for small files) followed by single, double and triple indirect pointers that point to blocks of pointers. Small files stay quick, while huge files remain possible. Check the quiz answer yourself: 4 KiB blocks and 4-byte pointers give 1024 pointers per indirect block. With 12 direct and one single-indirect pointer that's (12 + 1024) × 4 KiB = 4,144 KiB. Switch on double-indirect and the maximum jumps to about 4 GiB.","Lesson"],
  ["modules/11-file-systems.html#links","Module 11: File Systems","Links","Hard link: another directory entry pointing to the same inode. The inode keeps a link count; data is freed when it reaches 0. Cannot cross file systems. Symbolic link: a small file containing a path. It can cross file systems and point to directories, but dangles if the target goes away. Try it: hard links, symbolic links and inodes Why C is the clearer version here: link, symlink, unlink and stat are the system calls themselves, and st_ino and st_nlink are the inode fields the lesson describes. Python’s os module mirrors the calls one-to-one (os.link, os.symlink, os.unlink, os.stat), so this is no more contrived than the C version. It needs Linux or macOS: on Windows, hard links exist but behave differently and symbolic links usually need extra privileges.","Lesson"],
  ["modules/11-file-systems.html#free-space-and-reliability","Module 11: File Systems","Free space and reliability","Free-space tracking: bitmaps (1 bit per block) or free lists/extents. Crash consistency: a single logical operation (creating a file) touches several structures (bitmap, inode, directory). A crash in the middle leaves them inconsistent. Old systems ran fsck over the whole disk; modern file systems use journaling (write-ahead log) or copy-on-write designs to recover quickly. Performance: the buffer cache, read-ahead, delayed writes and extents (ranges of blocks instead of single pointers, as in ext4 and NTFS). Layering: a Virtual File System (VFS) layer lets the same system calls work across ext4, NTFS, NFS and others. Practice the calculations: maximum file size problems (random problems with worked solutions). AdvancedGoing deeper: what modern file systems add Journaling. Without it a crash in the middle of an update can leave the structures inconsistent, so older systems had to scan the whole disk at boot (fsck). A journal records the intended change first, so recovery replays or discards it in seconds. Most systems journal only metadata by default, which keeps structure safe but not necessarily your data. Copy-on-write file systems. ZFS, Btrfs and APFS never overwrite live blocks. They write a new version and then switch a pointer, which makes snapshots nearly free and makes a torn write impossible. The cost is fragmentation and more work to track free space. Extents instead of block lists. Modern systems (ext4, NTFS, XFS) store a file as a few (start, length) ranges rather than one pointer per block, so a large contiguous file needs only a handful of entries and the classic indirect-block limits do not apply. fsync and lying disks. A successful write only means the data reached the page cache. Durability needs fsync, and database authors worry about drives that acknowledge writes before they are on stable media. Many “lost data after power failure” stories come from skipping this. Checksums and silent corruption. Disks sometimes return wrong data without an error. ZFS and Btrfs store a checksum with every block pointer and, with redundancy, repair what fails. Traditional systems trust the device. Directories are files too. A directory is a file whose contents map names to inode numbers, which is why a hard link is just another entry and why large directories use hash tables or B-trees to avoid slow linear searches.","Lesson"],
  ["modules/12-os-landscape.html","Module 12: Beyond Windows, macOS and Linux","Beyond Windows, macOS and Linux","Every operating system is a set of trade-offs shaped by what it is for. A phone, a car's dashboard, a bank's transaction engine and a journalist's laptop each push the same ideas (scheduling, memory, files, protection) in very different directions.","Lesson"],
  ["modules/12-os-landscape.html#pressures","Module 12: Beyond Windows, macOS and Linux","Why systems differ","The concepts from Modules 1–11 apply everywhere, but the priorities change. Compare what each kind of system optimizes for: Kind of systemMain goalWhat that does to the design Desktop / server (Windows, macOS, Linux)Run many different programs wellGeneral-purpose scheduling, large virtual memory, broad driver support Mobile (Android, iOS)Battery life, responsiveness, safe third-party appsStrict app sandboxes, the OS controls app lifecycle and may kill background apps, aggressive power management Security-focused (Qubes)Limit the damage when something is compromisedIsolation first: whole-VM compartments, convenience second Embedded / real-time (FreeRTOS, QNX)Meet deadlines predictably, with tiny resourcesFixed-priority scheduling, little or no virtual memory, minimal features Single-board / education (Raspberry Pi OS)Low cost, hackable, connect to the physical worldA general-purpose Linux plus easy access to hardware pins Mainframe (z/OS, z/VSE) and midrange (IBM i)Reliability, throughput and compatibility for business-critical workWorkload management, offloaded I/O, strong accounting and security, decades of backward compatibility","Lesson"],
  ["modules/12-os-landscape.html#tree","Module 12: Beyond Windows, macOS and Linux","Where did they come from?","Many systems are relatives. Android, ChromeOS and Raspberry Pi OS all share the Linux kernel; macOS and iOS share Apple's Darwin core. IBM's mainframe world has two parallel families (OS/360 and DOS/360), and its midrange line grew from a different idea altogether (System/38).","Lesson"],
  ["modules/12-os-landscape.html#compare","Module 12: Beyond Windows, macOS and Linux","Compare the systems","Add any systems to the comparison and read them side by side. Tip: after you choose systems, the address in your browser changes to something like #compare=windows,zos. Copy it to share your comparison with a classmate or turn it into a discussion prompt. The profiles are simplified summaries. Specific details (such as default schedulers or storage options) change from version to version, so check the vendor's documentation for exact, current facts.","Lesson"],
  ["modules/12-os-landscape.html#mobile","Module 12: Beyond Windows, macOS and Linux","Mobile: Android and iOS","Apps do not own the machine. The OS starts, pauses and kills apps to protect battery and memory. A mobile app must save its state whenever it is sent to the background, because it may never be resumed. Strong sandboxes. Each app is isolated from the others and gets only the permissions the user grants (camera, location, contacts). Same ideas, different kernels. Android uses a modified kernel from Linux; iOS uses Apple's XNU. Both rely on the hardware's user/kernel separation from Module 1. Memory pressure is handled by terminating apps rather than paging to a slow flash drive.","Lesson"],
  ["modules/12-os-landscape.html#secure","Module 12: Beyond Windows, macOS and Linux","Security-focused: Qubes OS","Most operating systems try to make one big system trustworthy. Qubes does the opposite and assumes something will be hacked, then limits the blast radius. A hypervisor runs separate virtual machines (“qubes”) for work, personal browsing, banking and untrusted downloads. A bug in a browser in the “untrusted” qube cannot read files in the “work” qube. This is least privilege applied to whole operating systems. The price is higher hardware needs and a more complex workflow, which is the central security versus usability trade-off.","Lesson"],
  ["modules/12-os-landscape.html#embedded","Module 12: Beyond Windows, macOS and Linux","Embedded and real-time","A real-time operating system guarantees that important work finishes on time, not that it finishes fast on average. That pushes the design toward predictable fixed-priority scheduling and away from features such as virtual memory, whose page faults make timing unpredictable. At the small end (FreeRTOS), the “OS” is a library compiled into the firmware on a microcontroller. At the larger end (QNX), a microkernel runs drivers as separate processes so that a driver failure in a car does not crash the whole system. Raspberry Pi OS sits between worlds: a full Linux system on a tiny, inexpensive board whose GPIO pins let your code blink LEDs or read sensors. It is excellent for learning but, being standard Linux, it is not a hard real-time system.","Lesson"],
  ["modules/12-os-landscape.html#mainframe","Module 12: Beyond Windows, macOS and Linux","Mainframe and midrange systems","Mainframes are not museum pieces. They process a very large share of the world's banking and airline transactions, and their operating systems reflect that job. A few ideas that look different from what you see on a PC: IdeaPC-style OSMainframe-style OS (z/OS, z/VSE) Typical workInteractive programsHuge batch runs (described in JCL) plus thousands of simultaneous online transactions I/OCPU drives devices via drivers and DMAA dedicated channel subsystem handles I/O so the CPUs stay busy computing Scheduling goalFairness and responsivenessMeet business goals (“payments must complete in 0.2 s”) through the Workload Manager Sharing the hardwareProcesses, maybe VMsLogical partitions (LPARs) in hardware, each running its own OS, plus z/VM for more virtual machines FilesHierarchical directoriesDatasets located by catalogs (and a UNIX file system alongside) Text encodingASCII / UnicodeEBCDIC traditionally, so data is converted when exchanged with other systems ReliabilityReboot occasionallyDesigned for continuous operation and clustering (Parallel Sysplex) CompatibilityOften broken by major upgradesPrograms from the 1960s still run, a core selling point z/OS descends from OS/360 and MVS. It is the large-scale flagship. z/VSE descends from the separate DOS/360 line: smaller, simpler and cheaper to operate, often used by smaller organizations. IBM i (formerly AS/400, iSeries, System i) is a midrange system with a database built into the OS, an object-based design, and single-level storage: memory and disk form one address space, so applications never manage paging or files the way they do on a PC. Connect it back: single-level storage is virtual memory taken to its logical extreme, and the channel subsystem is a more powerful version of the DMA idea.","Lesson"],
  ["modules/12-os-landscape.html#checklist","Module 12: Beyond Windows, macOS and Linux","Questions to ask about any new OS","What is it for, and what does it sacrifice to do that well? Where is the line between kernel and user space? Is it a monolithic kernel, a microkernel, or a hypervisor? How does it schedule work: fairness, priorities, deadlines, or business goals? Does it give each program its own virtual memory? What happens when memory runs short: swap, kill, or nothing? What is a “file” here, and how are names organized? What is the unit of isolation and trust: user, app, container, VM, partition? Who controls updates and software installation? AdvancedGoing deeper: design ideas worth knowing Unikernels. Link the application with only the OS libraries it needs and run the result directly on a hypervisor. There is no shell, no other process and almost nothing to attack, and the image can boot in milliseconds. Capability-based systems. seL4 and Fuchsia’s Zircon replace ambient authority (“run as user X”) with unforgeable handles: a program can use only what it was explicitly given. This is the access-control matrix stored by row, and seL4’s kernel is formally verified. Containers are not virtual machines. A container is ordinary processes using kernel namespaces (what they can see) and cgroups (what they can use), so all share one kernel. A VM runs its own kernel on virtual hardware, which isolates more strongly and costs more. Real-time operating systems. VxWorks, FreeRTOS and Zephyr guarantee a bounded response time rather than a fast average. Their schedulers are simple priority-based designs so that worst-case latency can be proven. Microkernels in production. QNX runs cars and medical devices, and Apple’s Secure Enclave runs an L4-family kernel. A fault in one driver restarts that driver, not the machine. Minix 3 even restarts failed drivers automatically.","Lesson"],
  ["modules/13-ipc.html","Module 13: Inter-Process Communication","Inter-Process Communication","Every process lives in its own protected address space, which is great for safety and terrible for cooperation. Inter-process communication (IPC) is the set of controlled openings the kernel provides so that processes can still work together.","Lesson"],
  ["modules/13-ipc.html#why","Module 13: Inter-Process Communication","Why processes need IPC","A shell pipeline such as ls | grep txt | wc -l runs three separate programs. A web server hands requests to worker processes. A browser keeps each tab in its own process and still has to draw them in one window. None of these programs can read another's variables, because the MMU and the page tables you met in Module 8 make each address space private. To share anything, they must ask the kernel.","Lesson"],
  ["modules/13-ipc.html#two","Module 13: Inter-Process Communication","Two families","Message passing: the sender calls a system call to hand data to the kernel, and the receiver calls another to get it. The kernel copies the data and also decides who must wait. Pipes, sockets and message queues work this way. It is easy to get right, and it works across machines. Shared memory: the kernel maps the same physical pages into both address spaces. After the set-up, data moves with ordinary loads and stores: no system call, no copy. The price is that the kernel is no longer in the middle, so the processes must synchronize themselves.","Lesson"],
  ["modules/13-ipc.html#pipes","Module 13: Inter-Process Communication","Pipes","A pipe is a buffer inside the kernel with two ends, each referred to by a file descriptor. One process writes into one end and another reads from the other end. Three rules explain almost everything about its behavior: It is a byte stream. The pipe does not remember where one write stopped and the next began. Two writes may be returned by one read, and one write by several reads. Waiting is built in. A write to a full pipe puts the writer to sleep, and a read from an empty pipe puts the reader to sleep. A sleeping process uses no CPU. This is the same producer–consumer idea as the bounded buffer in Module 6, with the kernel doing the locking. Ends close, and that means something. A read returns zero bytes, end of file, only when the buffer is empty and every write end is closed. A write when no read end remains is a broken pipe: the writer gets a signal (SIGPIPE) or an error. Try it: use the scenarios, then experiment. With a pipe, write hello and then world and read 20 bytes: you get helloworld in one read. Switch to a message queue and the same sequence needs two reads. Next, fill the pipe, and notice that the writer sleeps until the reader makes room. Finally close the writer's end while the reader is asleep. Try it: a pipe between a parent and its child Why C is the clearer version here: pipe, fork, read, write and close are the real system calls, and the two file descriptors are plain integers in an array. Look at comment (2): because fork copies the descriptor table, the child holds a write end it never uses, and forgetting to close it is the classic reason a reader never sees end of file. Python’s os module mirrors the C calls almost one-to-one (os.pipe, os.fork, os.read, os.write), so nothing here is contrived. The difference is convenience: higher-level tools such as subprocess and multiprocessing build and close the pipes for you, which is why Python programmers rarely write this code by hand. Needs Linux or macOS because of fork. An ordinary pipe has no name, so only related processes (a parent and children that inherit it) can use it. A named pipe (FIFO) appears in the file system, so unrelated processes can open it. It behaves the same way otherwise.","Lesson"],
  ["modules/13-ipc.html#sockets","Module 13: Inter-Process Communication","Sockets and message queues","A pipe is one-way. A socket is an endpoint that can both send and receive, and the same calls work whether the other end is a process on this machine (a Unix-domain socket) or a program on the other side of the world (a network socket). That is why sockets are the foundation of client/server software. If you need the boundaries between items preserved, use a message queue (POSIX mq_send and mq_receive, or datagram sockets): each receive returns exactly one message. Compare the two mechanisms in the explorer above. Try it: asking a question and getting an answer Why C is the clearer version here: socketpair returns two connected descriptors, exactly like pipe, except that either end can be read and written. Comment (4) shows the blocking rule from the pipe section again: the parent’s read sleeps until the child answers. Python wraps this nicely. The socket module’s socketpair() returns objects with sendall and recv, so the same idea takes fewer lines and no pointer arithmetic. A real server would use socket.socket(...) with bind, listen and accept, which is where the network version begins.","Lesson"],
  ["modules/13-ipc.html#shm","Module 13: Inter-Process Communication","Shared memory","With shared memory the kernel does the work once, when the mapping is created. Afterwards both processes just use the memory. This is the fastest local mechanism, and it is also the most dangerous, because two processes can now race on the same bytes exactly as two threads can. In practice shared memory is paired with a mutex or semaphore that lives inside the shared block itself. Try it: what the child can and cannot change Why C is the clearer version here: mmap with MAP_SHARED | MAP_ANONYMOUS shows precisely what is special about the block: ordinary memory is copied by fork (so the child’s change stays private), while the shared mapping points at the same physical pages in both processes. Python can do it, but it is a thinner abstraction. An anonymous mmap is shared with a forked child, but it holds bytes rather than typed values, so structured data needs struct or ctypes. In day-to-day Python you would use multiprocessing.shared_memory or multiprocessing.Value, which add the locking and cleanup that raw shared memory leaves to you.","Lesson"],
  ["modules/13-ipc.html#cost","Module 13: Inter-Process Communication","What each one costs","Try it: choose “Small messages”, where a pipe needs two system calls per message, then “One big transfer”. Shared memory’s advantage grows with the number of transfers, but it never removes the need to coordinate.","Lesson"],
  ["modules/13-ipc.html#choose","Module 13: Inter-Process Communication","Choosing a mechanism","MechanismDirection and reachMessage boundaries?Good for PipeOne way, related processesNo (byte stream)Shell pipelines, parent–child output Named pipe (FIFO)One way, any process on this machineNoSimple logging or command channels Unix-domain socketTwo way, this machineStream or datagramLocal services (databases, display servers, Docker) Network socketTwo way, any machineStream (TCP) or datagram (UDP)Client/server programs, the Internet Message queueAny processes on this machineYesTask hand-off with priorities Shared memoryAny processes on this machineUp to youLarge or very frequent transfers, with locking SignalOne way, any process you may signalNo data at all“Stop”, “reload”, “child finished” Ordinary fileAny process, even after a restartUp to youSlow, persistent hand-off AdvancedGoing deeper: how real systems do it Atomic small writes. POSIX guarantees that a write of up to PIPE_BUF bytes (at least 512, 4096 on Linux) is never interleaved with another process’s write to the same pipe. Larger writes may be split, so several writers sending big records into one pipe can garble them. Pipe capacity. Linux pipes hold 64 KiB by default (adjustable with fcntl). A writer that outruns its reader sleeps, and that back-pressure is why a shell pipeline never needs to buffer a whole file in memory. Zero-copy. Calls such as splice, sendfile and vmsplice hand page references between descriptors instead of copying bytes, which is how a web server can send a file from the disk cache to a socket without the data ever entering user space. Descriptors can themselves be passed. A Unix-domain socket can carry an open file descriptor to another process (SCM_RIGHTS), which is how a privileged helper opens a file and hands the result to an unprivileged program. This is the capability idea from Module 2 in practice. IPC in the systems of Module 12. Android’s apps talk through Binder, a kernel driver that delivers a call to another process and returns the result, with the caller’s identity attached for permission checks. macOS and iOS use Mach ports. Windows offers named pipes, ALPC and COM. Microkernels such as QNX are built almost entirely on fast message passing. Lock-free ring buffers. High-performance shared-memory channels use a circular buffer with one producer and one consumer, each advancing only its own index, so neither needs a lock. They depend on the memory-ordering rules from Module 6. Shared memory is still memory. It is mapped through page tables like everything els","Lesson"],
  ["modules/14-virtualization.html","Module 14: Virtualization and Containers","Virtualization and Containers","An operating system already makes one computer look like many: each process believes it has the CPU and its own memory. Virtualization pushes that idea further, so that a whole machine or a whole system environment can be copied, moved, limited and thrown away.","Lesson"],
  ["modules/14-virtualization.html#why","Module 14: Virtualization and Containers","Why virtualize?","Sharing hardware. Most servers sit mostly idle. Several isolated workloads on one machine use it far better. Isolation. A crash or compromise in one environment should not spread to its neighbors. Packaging and repeatability. An environment that is described exactly (the same libraries, the same settings) behaves the same on a laptop and in a data center. Mobility. If a workload is a bundle of state, it can be moved, copied, snapshotted and rolled back. There are two main answers, and they sit at different levels. A virtual machine virtualizes the hardware, so each environment runs its own whole operating system. A container virtualizes the operating system, so every environment shares one kernel but sees a private view of it. Try it: start four applications with each approach and watch the kernel count. Containers stay at one. Virtual machines pay for one kernel per VM. In the cloud design, a container’s neighbors share a kernel only if they belong to the same customer’s VM.","Lesson"],
  ["modules/14-virtualization.html#vm","Module 14: Virtualization and Containers","Virtual machines","A virtual machine (VM) is a complete, software-defined computer: virtual CPUs, memory, disks and network cards. A hypervisor (also called a virtual machine monitor) creates VMs and shares the real hardware among them. A type 1 hypervisor (Xen, ESXi, Hyper-V, or KVM inside Linux) is the lowest layer of software. A type 2 hypervisor (the desktop virtualization programs) runs as an ordinary program on a host OS. How the CPU is shared The guest operating system believes it runs in kernel mode and may execute privileged instructions, but the hypervisor cannot allow that, or the guest could take over the machine. Early virtualization used trap-and-emulate: the guest actually runs in user mode, the first privileged instruction traps (as in Module 1), and the hypervisor emulates what the instruction would have done and resumes the guest. Some older CPUs had instructions that misbehaved silently instead of trapping, so products rewrote the guest’s code on the fly. Modern CPUs (Intel VT-x, AMD-V, ARM virtualization extensions) add a dedicated guest mode, so most guest code runs at full speed and the hardware does the switch for you. How memory is shared The guest has its own page tables mapping guest-virtual addresses to what it thinks is physical memory. The hypervisor adds another level of translation behind it (nested or extended page tables), which is the “virtual machines pay twice” point from Module 8. The hypervisor can then give each VM a different slice of real RAM, or even promise more than exists. How I/O is shared Pretending to be a real network card is simple but slow, because each poke at a pretend register stops the guest: a VM exit. Paravirtualized devices such as virtio trade the pretence for cooperation: the guest knows it is a guest, queues many requests in memory it shares with the hypervisor, and exits once per batch. Try it: send 1,000 packets, then set the batch size to 1 and watch virtio lose most of its advantage. Then raise the exit cost: the more expensive an exit is, the more batching and passthrough matter.","Lesson"],
  ["modules/14-virtualization.html#containers","Module 14: Virtualization and Containers","Containers","A container is not a new kernel feature. It is an ordinary process that the kernel has been asked to restrict in two ways: what it can see, and how much it can use. Add a packaged file tree (a container image) as its root directory and it looks like a small private computer. What it can see is controlled by namespaces. There is one for process IDs, one for the file system tree, one for the network, and so on. A process always belongs to one namespace of each kind; a container runtime simply starts it in new ones. Try it: start with “Ordinary process” and tick one namespace at a time. Notice that each one changes only its own command. Process isolation is built from independent pieces, which is why runtimes can mix them (for example a pod of containers that share one network namespace). Then try the “Only PID” preset: the process sees only itself, but still shares the host’s network and files. Try it: every process is already in namespaces Why C is the clearer version here: the kernel exposes namespaces as symbolic links in /proc/<pid>/ns/, and readlink shows the identifier of each one. Two processes with the same identifier share that namespace. The example shows that every process has one of each kind, and that fork copies the memberships. A container runtime starts its child with clone or unshare flags such as CLONE_NEWPID instead, which is the only difference. Python can do this directly with os.readlink, so it is no more contrived than the C version, and the comparison of the two dictionaries is shorter. Creating new namespaces is possible too (os.unshare arrived in Python 3.12), but it needs elevated privileges, so this example only looks. Linux only: /proc does not exist on macOS or Windows. Because the kernel is shared, a container is limited to what that kernel can run. A Linux container needs a Linux kernel. That is why Docker on a Mac or Windows laptop quietly starts a small Linux virtual machine and runs all containers in it.","Lesson"],
  ["modules/14-virtualization.html#cgroups","Module 14: Virtualization and Containers","Limits: control groups","Namespaces do not stop one container from eating all the memory. That is the job of control groups (cgroups), which let the kernel limit and account for the CPU, memory, disk I/O and number of processes used by a group. Compare the CPU controls to the scheduling algorithms in Module 4: weights give proportional shares under contention (as in a fair-share scheduler), while a cap is a hard limit. Try it: set the cap on the batch job to none and watch it take its share. Then make the web group ask for just one core and see the others receive the leftover. That is what work-conserving means. Finally put a cap on a group when the machine is nearly idle: the cap holds it back anyway. Try it: lower the limit until the database no longer fits. Memory is different from CPU: a group that wants too much CPU is only slowed down, but memory cannot be taken back from a running program, so something has to die. Try it: a limit enforced by the kernel Why C is the clearer version here: setrlimit is the older, per-process cousin of a cgroup limit, and it shows the key behavior: the kernel enforces the limit, the allocation request fails, and the program can notice and carry on. A cgroup memory limit is the same idea applied to a group, except that it can also kill. (The 1024 MiB figure is the limit; the 2048 MiB request is refused. Linux only, because other systems do not enforce RLIMIT_AS the same way.) The same call is available as resource.setrlimit. In Python the refusal shows up as an exception (OSError or MemoryError) instead of a special return value (MAP_FAILED) that C code must remember to check. Python programs seldom set these limits themselves; a container runtime or the shell’s ulimit does it for them.","Lesson"],
  ["modules/14-virtualization.html#compare","Module 14: Virtualization and Containers","VM or container?","The trade-off is strength of isolation against weight. A container starts in a fraction of a second and costs little memory, but a bug in the shared kernel is a bug for every container on it. A VM puts a hypervisor, a much smaller interface than the whole system-call table, between the tenants, at the price of a kernel per VM. Security-sensitive platforms therefore combine them: containers for packaging and speed, a VM boundary between customers. Optional, on a Linux machine with root access (the site’s tests do not run this): sudo unshare --pid --fork --mount-proc ps -e starts ps in new PID and mount namespaces. It lists only itself, as PID 1. That is the “Only PID” view from the simulator on your own system. AdvancedGoing deeper: how the pieces really fit Image layers. A container image is a stack of read-only layers (each one a set of file changes) combined by a union file system such as overlayfs, with a thin writable layer on top. Ten containers from one image share the lower layers on disk and in the page cache, which is much of why they are cheap. Capabilities and system-call filters. Container runtimes drop most of root’s powers (see Module 2) and install a seccomp filter listing which system calls are allowed. A user namespace can also map “root inside” to an unprivileged ID on the host, which is how rootless containers work. MicroVMs and sandboxes. Firecracker boots a minimal VM in roughly a tenth of a second with very few virtual devices, so each customer function gets a VM boundary at nearly container speed. gVisor and Kata Containers go the other ways: gVisor intercepts system calls in a user-space “kernel”, and Kata runs each pod in a lightweight VM. Hardware assists. Beyond VT-x there are nested page tables (EPT/NPT) for memory, an IOMMU to keep a device inside its owner’s memory, SR-IOV to split one network card into many virtual ones, and confidential-computing extensions that encrypt a VM’s memory even from the hypervisor. Live migration. A running VM can move between hosts with almost no pause: the hypervisor copies memory while the guest keeps running, tracks which pages get dirtied (using the page-table dirty bits from Module 9), re-copies them, and finally pauses the guest for a few milliseconds to move the last of its state. Memory overcommit in VMs. Hypervisors reclaim memory from idle guests with ballooning (a driver in the guest asks its own OS for pages and hands them back), share identical pages, and compress or swap as a last r","Lesson"],
  ["modules/15-monitors.html","Module 15: Monitors and Readers–Writers","Monitors and Readers–Writers","Semaphores can solve any synchronization problem, but they are easy to get wrong: swap two calls, forget one, and the program deadlocks or races. This module covers the tools that real programs use instead: the monitor with its condition variables, and the read-write lock, which shows that even “who goes first?” is a design decision with consequences.","Lesson"],
  ["modules/15-monitors.html#monitor","Module 15: Monitors and Readers–Writers","Monitors and condition variables","Recall the bounded buffer from Module 6: it needed one semaphore as a mutex and two more to count empty and full slots, and the order of the calls mattered. A monitor packages the same ideas more safely. It bundles the shared data, the lock that protects it, and one or more condition variables on which threads can sleep until something they need has happened. Because the data can only be touched through the monitor’s own operations, the locking discipline lives in one place instead of in every caller. A condition variable has three operations, always used while holding the lock: wait: atomically release the lock and go to sleep. When woken, reacquire the lock before returning. Doing both at once is essential: if a thread released the lock, then slept, a signal sent in between would be lost forever. signal (notify): wake one sleeping thread, if there is one. Signaling with no one waiting does nothing. A condition variable does not remember signals, unlike a semaphore, which counts them. broadcast (notify all): wake every sleeping thread, for when the change may help several of them.","Lesson"],
  ["modules/15-monitors.html#mesa","Module 15: Monitors and Readers–Writers","Why “while”, not “if”","When a thread signals, who runs next? In the textbook’s original design (Hoare semantics) the signaler hands the lock straight to the waiter, so the condition is guaranteed true when the waiter wakes. That is hard to implement efficiently, and real systems do something simpler, called Mesa semantics after the language where it was first used: the signal only makes the waiter ready. The signaler keeps the lock, and the waiter must queue for it like anyone else. In the gap, another thread can get in and change the data. The wake-up is a hint that the condition may be true, not a promise that it still is. (Some systems also deliver a spurious wakeup, a return from wait that nobody signaled, which the specification permits.) The rule that follows is simple: always wait inside a loop that re-tests the condition. Try it: step through the if version. C1 finds the buffer empty and sleeps. P1 adds an item and signals, but keeps the lock. Before C1 gets the lock back, C2 arrives and takes the item. C1 then continues as if the item were still there. Switch to while and run the same steps: C1 re-checks and sleeps again. The random runs show that this is not a freak case.","Lesson"],
  ["modules/15-monitors.html#queue","Module 15: Monitors and Readers–Writers","A bounded queue, done properly","Here is the producer–consumer problem with a monitor-style design: one lock, and two condition variables (“not full” for producers to sleep on, “not empty” for consumers). Notice that every wait is in a loop, and that each side signals the other condition. Try it: two producers, two consumers Why C is the clearer version here: pthread_cond_wait(&cond, &lock) takes the mutex as an argument, which makes the atomic “release and sleep” visible (comment 3). The final broadcast shows a detail beginners miss: consumers asleep on an empty queue must be woken when the work is over, or the program never exits (comment 5). Python’s threading.Condition is the same idea with less ceremony. A with block takes and releases the lock, and wait() releases it while sleeping. In practice you would use queue.Queue, which is exactly this code packaged up, so a hand-written version is mostly for understanding. The global interpreter lock does not make the loops unnecessary: a thread can still lose its turn between the signal and its wake-up.","Lesson"],
  ["modules/15-monitors.html#rw","Module 15: Monitors and Readers–Writers","Readers and writers","Many programs read shared data far more often than they change it: a routing table, a configuration, a cache. Two readers cannot interfere with each other, so making them take turns wastes parallelism. The readers–writers problem is to let readers share while a writer is kept alone. The usual tool is a read–write lock with two modes: shared and exclusive. That leaves a policy question for when both kinds are waiting: Reader preference. A reader never waits behind another reader, even if a writer is already waiting. Maximum sharing, but a steady stream of readers can keep a writer out indefinitely. Writer preference. When a writer is waiting, new readers wait too. Data stays fresh, but a steady stream of writers can starve the readers. First come, first served. Requests are admitted in arrival order, and a run of readers at the front can go together. Nobody starves, at the cost of some sharing. Try it: the first workload is a steady stream of readers plus a single writer that arrives early. Under reader preference it waits for the entire stream. Switch to writer preference, and then to first come, first served. Then pick the second workload (a stream of writers and one reader) and see the roles reverse. The comparison table shows that no policy wins every column. Try it: readers that always see a consistent pair Why C is the clearer version here: POSIX has a read-write lock built in (pthread_rwlock_t), with rdlock for shared and wrlock for exclusive access. The writer changes two variables that must always match; a reader that holds the lock can rely on them matching, which is exactly what the lock buys. This is more contrived in Python. The standard library has no read–write lock, so the example builds one from a Condition, and it is a plain reader-preference lock, so it can starve a writer. Also, because of the global interpreter lock, Python readers never run in parallel anyway, so the speed benefit that motivates a read-write lock mostly disappears. The code is useful for seeing how such a lock works, not as something to copy into a program.","Lesson"],
  ["modules/15-monitors.html#langs","Module 15: Monitors and Readers–Writers","In real languages","LanguageMonitor / condition variableRead–write lock C (POSIX)pthread_mutex_t + pthread_cond_tpthread_rwlock_t Javasynchronized with wait() / notify(), or ReentrantLock + ConditionReentrantReadWriteLock, StampedLock Pythonthreading.Condition, or queue.Queuenone in the standard library C++std::mutex + std::condition_variablestd::shared_mutex Gochannels (and sync.Cond)sync.RWMutex RustMutex + Condvar (the data lives inside the mutex)RwLock AdvancedGoing deeper: costs and alternatives Hoare versus Mesa, precisely. In Hoare’s version the signal transfers the lock and the condition immediately, so an if is enough, but it forces an extra context switch on every signal. Mesa lets the signaler continue, which allows the cheaper “signal, then keep working” pattern, at the price of the loop. Java, POSIX, Python and C++ are all Mesa-style. Signal or broadcast? Signaling one waiter is only safe when any waiter can use the change. If waiters want different things (a buffer of unequal-size requests, say), signaling one may wake a thread that cannot proceed and leave the right one asleep. broadcast is always correct; it can be slower because everyone wakes and re-checks (a thundering herd). Lost wake-ups. If a program checks a condition without holding the lock and then sleeps, a signal arriving between the two steps is lost, and the thread may sleep forever. This is the reason waiting and the lock release are one atomic operation. Read–write locks are not free. Even shared readers must update the lock’s counter, which bounces a cache line between cores. With short read sections, a plain mutex or a lock-free design can beat a read–write lock. Linux uses RCU for read-mostly kernel data, so readers do not write to shared memory at all. Upgrading is dangerous. A thread holding a read lock cannot simply ask for the write lock: two readers both trying to upgrade will each wait for the other to leave, a deadlock (see Module 7). Most libraries do not support upgrade for this reason. Seqlocks. For small, frequently read data, a writer increments a version number before and after updating, and a reader retries if the number changed or was odd. Readers never block the writer and never take a lock, which solves writer starvation by construction. Fairness versus throughput. Fair queues can create a lock convoy: every thread wakes, runs a moment, and sleeps again behind the next. Real implementations mix policies, for example letting a thread barge in once but putting it at the back a","Lesson"],
  ["modules/16-boot.html","Module 16: The Boot Process","The Boot Process","The operating system lives on a disk, but to read a disk you need an operating system. Booting is the answer to that chicken-and-egg problem: a chain of small programs, each just capable enough to load the next, until the kernel is running and can take over.","Lesson"],
  ["modules/16-boot.html#stages","Module 16: The Boot Process","The stages","When power arrives, the CPU does the only thing it can: it starts executing at a fixed address, where the machine’s firmware lives in a chip that keeps its contents without power. From there the work is handed on: Firmware tests and initializes the hardware, then looks for something bootable. On older PCs this is the BIOS; newer machines use UEFI. The boot loader (GRUB, systemd-boot, Windows Boot Manager) is found on disk, run, and asked which kernel to start. The kernel is loaded into memory along with an initial RAM file system, and begins running. This is the moment the operating system, as Module 1 describes it, comes into existence. The kernel mounts the real root file system and starts one program: init, process number 1, which starts everything else. Try it: play the three firmware types and compare them. Then break things. “Boot sector missing” stops a BIOS machine at stage 3 but a UEFI machine at a different stage, and a tampered boot loader stops only the Secure Boot machine; on the others it runs without complaint. Notice which failures leave you with a working kernel (an emergency shell) and which leave you with nothing.","Lesson"],
  ["modules/16-boot.html#mbr","Module 16: The Boot Process","The master boot record","A BIOS cannot read a file system, so it uses the one thing it can: sector numbers. It reads the very first sector of the disk (512 bytes) into memory at address 0x7C00 and jumps to it, provided the last two bytes are 55 AA. That sector, the master boot record, has a strictly defined layout: BytesContents 0 – 445Boot code (446 bytes: just enough to load something bigger) 446 – 509Four 16-byte partition entries: bootable flag, old CHS addresses, partition type, start sector, number of sectors 510 – 511The signature 0x55 0xAA Try it: change the start sector of partition 1 and watch the four bytes move. Enter 2048 and you will see 00 08 00 00: the number 0x0800 stored least significant byte first. Then damage the signature. UEFI machines use a GUID Partition Table (GPT) instead, which lifts the four-partition limit and the 2 TiB size limit that 32-bit sector numbers impose (232 sectors × 512 bytes), and keeps a backup copy at the end of the disk. A “protective MBR” (type 0xEE) is still written at the start so old tools do not mistake the disk for empty. Try it: build and read a master boot record Why C is the clearer version here: the sector is a plain array of bytes, and C makes you place each field yourself, which is exactly what boot code and file-system code do. The helper that stores a 32-bit number one byte at a time (comment 1) is the little-endian rule written out. Nothing here needs an operating system feature, so it runs on Windows, macOS and Linux. Python’s struct module does the packing for you. The format string \"<B3sB3sII\" describes one entry: a byte, three bytes, a byte, three bytes, then two 4-byte numbers, with < meaning little-endian. That is shorter and less error-prone than the C version, but it hides the byte-by-byte work that the C code makes visible. Real tools in Python use the same module to read raw disk structures.","Lesson"],
  ["modules/16-boot.html#init","Module 16: The Boot Process","Starting services","Once the kernel starts init, boot becomes a scheduling problem. Dozens of services must start, but a database cannot start before its disk is mounted and the network is up. An init system such as systemd is told what each service comes after and then starts every service as soon as its prerequisites have finished. That is a dependency graph, and the schedule is the same longest-path idea used for project planning: the critical path decides how long boot takes. Try it: make cron slower and nothing changes, because it is off the critical path. Make network slower and the whole boot takes longer. Then tick the mistake box: if the network waits for the web server, and the web server waits for the network, neither can ever start. This is the same circular-wait condition that causes deadlock, found before the system has finished starting. Try it: ordering services Why C is the clearer version here: the algorithm needs only arrays and a loop, and the code shows what the init system does at heart: repeatedly start every unit whose prerequisites are finished, and stop with an error if a pass starts nothing (comment 4), which means a cycle. It runs on any operating system. The Python version is shorter because dictionaries, list comprehensions and sorted with a key replace the lookup function, the manual sort and the string buffer. The logic is identical, so every numbered comment lines up with the C tab. This is a case where Python is the better tool: there is no memory or system-level detail to show.","Lesson"],
  ["modules/16-boot.html#secure","Module 16: The Boot Process","Secure Boot","Anything that runs before the operating system can control it, and the antivirus program is not loaded yet. A boot kit that replaces the boot loader owns the machine in a way no later software can detect. Secure Boot closes this by making every stage verify a digital signature on the next one before running it, forming a chain of trust that starts in the firmware, which the operating system cannot rewrite. Try it: tamper with the kernel and the chain breaks there, with the modules never reached. Tamper with the boot loader and the kernel, which is unchanged, is also untrusted: its signature was never checked by anything trustworthy. The strength of the chain is the strength of its weakest link, and of the keys at its root.","Lesson"],
  ["modules/16-boot.html#others","Module 16: The Boot Process","Other kinds of machine","The pattern, a chain of ever-more-capable loaders, is universal. The details vary, as the systems in Module 12 show: Raspberry Pi: the graphics processor, not the CPU, starts first, running code from its own ROM that reads boot files from the SD card before the main CPU starts at all. Phones: a ROM boot loader verifies the next stage, which verifies the next, up to the Android or iOS kernel (verified boot). Unlocking the bootloader deliberately breaks the chain. Mainframes: an IPL (initial program load) reads a loader from a designated device and starts the operating system, often virtualized under a hypervisor. Servers and thin clients: network boot (PXE) fetches the boot loader and kernel over the network, so a machine needs no local disk. AdvancedGoing deeper: what else happens at boot Measured boot and the TPM. Secure Boot refuses bad code; measured boot records what ran. Each stage hashes the next into a register of the Trusted Platform Module, which can only be extended, never set. A remote server can then ask the TPM to sign the result and decide whether to trust the machine (attestation). Device trees and ACPI. A kernel has to learn what hardware exists. PCs describe it through ACPI tables from the firmware; ARM boards usually pass a device tree, a data structure listing every device and its address, to the kernel at boot. Firmware is software too. UEFI is millions of lines of code, and other processors (the management engine, the baseband in a phone) run their own firmware before and beside the operating system. Projects such as coreboot replace the proprietary firmware with something small and auditable. The kernel command line. The loader passes a string such as root=/dev/sda2 quiet that tells the kernel where to find its root file system and how to behave. Editing it at the boot menu is the classic way to recover a machine, and the reason physical access usually means game over unless the disk is encrypted. Hibernation and fast start. Instead of booting, a machine can write all of memory to disk and later read it back (hibernate). Fast-startup features on laptops save only the kernel’s state, which makes the next boot quicker but means “shut down” was not a real shutdown. Finding the slow part. systemd-analyze blame and systemd-analyze critical-chain list the services that took longest and the dependency chain that determined the total, which is the critical path from the simulator above, measured on a real machine. kexec and live updates. Linu","Lesson"],
  ["modules/17-advanced-scheduling.html","Module 17: Advanced Scheduling","Advanced Scheduling","The algorithms in Module 4 assume that the scheduler knows how long each job will run, that all that matters is average speed, and that there is one CPU. Real systems break all three assumptions. This module covers the answers: queues that learn which jobs are interactive, schedulers that guarantee deadlines, and schedulers that spread work across many cores.","Lesson"],
  ["modules/17-advanced-scheduling.html#mlfq","Module 17: Advanced Scheduling","Learning from behavior: feedback queues","Shortest-job-first gives the best average waiting time, but it needs each job’s run time in advance, which an operating system never has. The multilevel feedback queue (MLFQ) approximates it by watching what jobs do. A handful of rules are enough: A new job starts in the top queue. The scheduler runs a job from the highest non-empty queue (round robin inside a queue). A higher queue always preempts a lower one. A job that uses its whole time slice is probably CPU-bound, so it moves down one queue. (Lower queues typically have longer slices, so the long jobs switch less often.) A job that gives up the CPU before its slice ends (to wait for a key press or a disk read) is probably interactive, so it keeps its level. Every so often, a priority boost moves every job back to the top, so nothing starves and a job that changes behavior is noticed. Try it: in the first workload, watch the long job (long) sink to the lowest queue while the short interactive jobs stay at the top and respond immediately. Now switch to the second workload, a stream of short jobs, and notice that the long job gets no CPU for a long stretch. Turn on a priority boost every 6 ticks: the long job gets a share. The cost is that the short jobs now finish a little later. Every setting is a trade-off between responsiveness and fairness. The scheduler can be fooled. A program that issues a tiny read just before its slice ends looks interactive forever, and so keeps the top queue while hogging the CPU. This is why real implementations count the total CPU time used at a level rather than only the last burst.","Lesson"],
  ["modules/17-advanced-scheduling.html#rt","Module 17: Advanced Scheduling","Meeting deadlines: real-time scheduling","A real-time system is correct only if it finishes work on time. A brake controller or an audio buffer cannot be “on average fast”. The usual model is a set of periodic tasks: task i releases a job every Ti ticks, each job needs Ci ticks of CPU, and it must finish before the next release, its deadline. The load the tasks place on the CPU is their utilization, U = Σ Ci/Ti. If U is more than 1 no algorithm can succeed. The question is how close to 1 each algorithm can go. Rate-monotonic: fixed priorities, shortest period highest. Simple and predictable. It is guaranteed to work when U ≤ n(21/n − 1), which is 0.828 for two tasks and falls toward 0.693 as tasks are added. Above that it may still work; the bound is sufficient, not necessary. Earliest deadline first: priorities change as time passes; the job with the nearest deadline runs. On one CPU it is optimal: it meets every deadline exactly when U ≤ 1. Try it: the first task set has U = 0.97. Under rate-monotonic, T2 is preempted by T1 at tick 5 and finishes at tick 8, one tick after its deadline of 7. EDF notices that T2’s deadline is nearer and finishes both. Then try “U = 1.00”, where the periods are multiples of each other (harmonic), and rate-monotonic reaches 100% too. Finally, push the utilization above 1 and watch every algorithm fail. Try it: simulate both algorithms Why C is the clearer version here: the scheduler is a handful of comparisons per tick, and C shows that the only difference between the two algorithms is the rule in comments (1) and (2) that chooses the next job. Real real-time kernels are written in C for the same reason: the cost of each decision is visible and bounded. (The root for the guarantee is found by bisection, so no math library is linked.) The Python version is the shorter one, because min(..., key=...) expresses “pick the job with the smallest (period, task, release)” in one line. For a simulation like this one that is a good fit. For an actual real-time system Python would be a poor choice: garbage collection and the interpreter make execution times hard to bound, and bounded timing is the whole point. Deadlines add a sharp edge to the synchronization problems of Module 6. If a low-priority task holds a lock that a high-priority task needs, and a medium-priority task then preempts the lock holder, the high-priority task is delayed by a task that is less important than it. This is priority inversion, and real-time systems fix it with priority inheritance: the lock holder","Lesson"],
  ["modules/17-advanced-scheduling.html#multi","Module 17: Advanced Scheduling","Many cores","With several CPUs the question changes from “which job next?” to “which job where?”. The usual design gives each core its own run queue, because one shared queue would need a lock that every core fights over. But separate queues can become unbalanced: one core has a backlog while another idles. Load balancing moves tasks between queues. In work stealing, an idle core takes a waiting task from a busy core’s queue. The catch is processor affinity: a task that has been running on one core has its data in that core’s cache. Move it and it starts cold, running slowly while the new cache fills. A balancer therefore weighs the benefit of using an idle core against the cost of the move. Try it: keep “all queued on core 0” and compare no balancing with work stealing. Then change the workload to one big task and many small ones: even with perfect balancing nothing can finish before the big task does, which is what the “best possible” figure shows. Finally raise the cost of a move and compare stealing with dealing tasks out evenly. Try it: the knobs on a real system Why C is the clearer version here: nice, getpriority and sched_setaffinity are the actual calls, and a CPU set is a small bit array manipulated with the CPU_SET family of macros. This is exactly what tools such as taskset and nice do on your behalf. Linux only (sched_setaffinity is a Linux call). Python exposes the same calls in the os module (os.nice, os.sched_getaffinity, os.sched_setaffinity) and uses an ordinary set of integers instead of a bit array, so it is no more contrived than the C version and a good deal tidier. As in C, they exist only on Linux.","Lesson"],
  ["modules/17-advanced-scheduling.html#linux","Module 17: Advanced Scheduling","What Linux actually does","PolicyForHow it chooses SCHED_OTHER (the default)Ordinary programsFair sharing: the task that has had the least CPU time relative to its weight runs next (the CFS and EEVDF schedulers). Niceness changes the weight. Works like a feedback queue in spirit, without explicit levels. SCHED_FIFO, SCHED_RRReal-time tasksFixed priorities 1–99, above all ordinary tasks. FIFO runs until it blocks; RR adds time slices among equals. This is rate-monotonic-style scheduling, with the priorities assigned by the programmer. SCHED_DEADLINETasks with explicit deadlinesEarliest deadline first, with an admission test: the kernel refuses a new task if the total utilization would go above what it can guarantee. Per-CPU run queues, load balancingMulticoreEach core has its own queue. Idle cores pull work, and a periodic balancer evens out loads, preferring moves within a cache domain or NUMA node. cgroup cpu.weight and cpu.maxContainersThe shares and caps from Module 14, applied to groups of tasks. AdvancedGoing deeper: limits of the models Where the 69% comes from. The Liu and Layland bound n(21/n − 1) is the worst case over all possible task sets. As n grows it tends to ln 2 ≈ 0.693. Many task sets with “awkward” periods fail below it, but most real sets (and every harmonic set) can go much higher, which is why exact response-time analysis, as in the table above, is used in practice. Response-time analysis. For fixed priorities, a task’s worst response time R is its own C plus the interference from higher-priority tasks that release during R: R = C + Σ ⌈R/Tj⌉ Cj. Solving it by repeated substitution gives an exact schedulability test for rate-monotonic scheduling. EDF’s weakness. EDF is optimal but fragile under overload: when it cannot meet all deadlines it can miss many, because it keeps preferring jobs that are about to be late. Fixed-priority schedulers fail more gracefully, since the low-priority tasks miss and the high-priority ones are untouched. Many safety-critical systems accept lower utilization for that predictability. Multiprocessor real time. On several CPUs, the optimal-on-one-CPU result no longer holds. Global EDF can miss deadlines even at low utilization (Dhall’s effect), so systems often partition tasks onto cores and run rate-monotonic or EDF on each. Graham’s bound. Any scheduler that never leaves a core idle while work is waiting finishes within (2 − 1/m) times the best possible time on m cores. The simulator’s “best possible” figure is the lower bound used ","Lesson"],
  ["cheatsheet.html","Cheat Sheet","Cheat Sheet","Formulas, quick tables and handy commands.","Cheat sheet"],
  ["cheatsheet.html#formulas","Cheat Sheet","Formulas","TopicFormula Turnaround timecompletion − arrival Waiting timeturnaround − burst Response timefirst run − arrival Context-switch efficiencyq / (q + s) Amdahl's law1 / ((1 − p) + p/N); limit 1/(1 − p) Page number / offsetpage = addr ÷ pageSize, offset = addr mod pageSize Physical addressframe × pageSize + offset Page-table entries2address bits / pageSize Internal fragmentationpages × pageSize − process size EAT with page faults(1 − p)·tmem + p·tfault EAT with TLBh·(ttlb + tm) + (1 − h)·(ttlb + 2·tm) Disk access timeseek + rotational latency + transfer; avg. rotational latency = ½ × 60/RPM Pointers per indirect blockblockSize / pointerSize Max file size (inode)(direct + k + k² + k³) × blockSize where k = pointers per block Banker's needNeed = Max − Allocation","Cheat sheet"],
  ["cheatsheet.html#powers-of-two","Cheat Sheet","Powers of two","PowerValuePowerValue 2101 KiB (1,024)2301 GiB 2124 KiB (typical page)2324 GiB 21664 KiB2401 TiB 2201 MiB248256 TiB (x86-64 virtual)","Cheat sheet"],
  ["cheatsheet.html#unix-permissions","Cheat Sheet","Unix permissions","OctalSymbolicTypical use 755rwxr-xr-xPrograms, public directories 644rw-r--r--Ordinary files 600rw-------Private files (SSH keys) 700rwx------Private directories 1777rwxrwxrwt/tmp (sticky bit)","Cheat sheet"],
  ["cheatsheet.html#python-threads-and-a-lock","Cheat Sheet","Python: threads and a lock","Note: in some CPython versions the GIL hides many races for simple operations. The lock is still required for correctness.","Cheat sheet"],
  ["cheatsheet.html#common-signals","Cheat Sheet","Common signals","SignalNumberMeaning SIGINT2Interrupt (Ctrl+C) SIGKILL9Kill immediately (cannot be caught) SIGSEGV11Invalid memory reference (segmentation fault) SIGTERM15Polite termination request SIGCHLD17 (Linux)A child process changed state","Cheat sheet"],
  ["cheatsheet.html#algorithms-at-a-glance","Cheat Sheet","Algorithms at a glance","AreaAlgorithmsRemember CPU schedulingFCFS, SJF, SRTF, RR, Priority, MLFQSJF minimizes average wait; RR trades overhead for response; add aging to stop starvation Memory placementFirst-, best-, worst-fitFirst-fit is usually the best compromise Page replacementFIFO, OPT, LRU, ClockFIFO can show Belady's anomaly; OPT is a benchmark Disk schedulingFCFS, SSTF, SCAN, C-SCAN, LOOKSSTF can starve; C-SCAN is uniform DeadlockPrevention, avoidance (Banker's), detection, ignoreBreak one of mutual exclusion, hold-and-wait, no preemption, circular wait","Cheat sheet"],
  ["labs/01-processes.html","Processes: launching, waiting and forking","Processes: launching, waiting and forking","Use Python to create and manage processes the way a shell does. You will see process IDs, exit codes, inherited environments and parallel children. On Linux or macOS you will also use fork, exec and wait directly and catch a zombie. About 45 minutesPart A: any systemPart B: Linux, macOS, WSL or CodioRelated: Module 3 Processes, Module 4 CPU Scheduling","Lab"],
  ["labs/01-processes.html#what-you-will-practice","Processes: launching, waiting and forking","What you will practice","Reading a process's id and its parent's id. Starting a child process, waiting for it, and reading its exit code. Seeing that a child inherits its parent's environment. Running several children at once and measuring the difference from running them one after another. Using fork, exec and wait, and recognizing a zombie process.","Lab"],
  ["labs/01-processes.html#part-a-launching-processes-any-system","Processes: launching, waiting and forking","Part A: launching processes (any system)","Predict first: if you start four children that each sleep for half a second, how long will your program take if they run one after another? If they run at the same time? Run it with python lab1_processes.py (on Windows you can also use py). Example output Process ids will be different on your machine. What to notice (1) Your own program is a process with a parent. The parent is whatever started it: your terminal, shell or editor. (2) subprocess.run starts a child and waits, like the shell does after fork and exec. The exit code 0 means success by convention. (3) Any non-zero exit code means failure, and the parent can read it. This is how scripts and build tools know a step failed. (4) The child received a copy of the parent's environment variables. Changing the copy would not change the parent's. This inheritance is part of what the kernel's process control block records. (5) Popen starts a child without waiting, so the four sleepers overlap and the total is about 0.5 s, not 2 s. Sleeping children use almost no CPU, which is why running more of them than you have cores still works.","Lab"],
  ["labs/01-processes.html#part-b-fork-exec-and-wait-linux-macos-wsl-or-codio","Processes: launching, waiting and forking","Part B: fork, exec and wait (Linux, macOS, WSL or Codio)","Windows has no os.fork(), so the script exits with a message there. Run Part B in WSL, in Codio, or on a Linux or macOS computer. Predict first: the program calls fork() three times in a row. How many processes exist at the end? (Check the fork tree in Module 3.) C is the natural language for this part. fork, exec and wait are C library functions, and Python's os.fork is a thin wrapper around them. Build and run it with gcc -std=c99 -D_POSIX_C_SOURCE=200809L -o lab1_fork lab1_fork.c && ./lab1_fork on Linux, macOS, WSL or Codio. The output is identical to the Python version. Python's os.fork, os.execvp and os.waitpid make the same system calls, so the behavior and the output are identical to the C tab. os._exit (like C's _exit) ends the child without Python's normal cleanup, which is what you want right after fork. Run the tab you prefer: python3 lab1_fork.py or the C build shown in the C tab. They print the same lines. Example output (from a Linux system) What to notice (1) fork() is called once but returns twice: 0 in the new child and the child's pid in the parent. That is how the same code tells the two apart. (2) waitpid blocks the parent until the child ends, then returns its exit status. Without it you get a zombie (see (5)). (3) exec does not create a process. It replaces the current program with another one in the same process. A shell runs a command with fork then exec in the child and wait in the parent. (4) Three forks in a row make 23 = 8 processes, because every process that exists runs the next fork() too. (5) A finished child stays in the process table as a zombie (state Z) until its parent collects its exit status. Zombies use almost no memory but they do use a process-table slot. Why the script flushes every print: fork copies the process, including text waiting in its output buffer. Without flushing, that text can be printed twice or out of order, especially when output goes to a file or pipe.","Lab"],
  ["labs/01-processes.html#try-it","Processes: launching, waiting and forking","Try it","In Part B change range(3) to range(4). Predict the number of processes, then run it. In Part A, change range(4) in step (5) to range(8). Does the total time change? Why or why not? In Part A, change step (5) so that each child is started and waited for before the next one starts (use subprocess.run instead of Popen). Compare the total time. In Part B, delete the os.waitpid(pid, 0) in step (5). What does ps show if you check after the program ends? (The orphaned zombie is adopted and reaped by the init process.) Make the child exit with a different code and confirm the parent reports it.","Lab"],
  ["labs/01-processes.html#common-mistakes","Processes: launching, waiting and forking","Common mistakes","Forgetting to wait. Children become zombies and you never see their exit codes. Forgetting that both processes run the code after fork(). Use if pid == 0 to separate them, and make the child exit so it does not continue into the parent's code. Calling fork in a program that already uses threads. Only the calling thread exists in the child, which can leave locks permanently held. Prefer subprocess or multiprocessing in threaded programs. Running Part B on Windows. There is no os.fork there. Use WSL, Codio or a Linux or macOS machine.","Lab"],
  ["labs/01-processes.html#reflection-questions-good-journal-entries","Processes: launching, waiting and forking","Reflection questions (good journal entries)","Explain in your own words why fork() returns two different values. What would be impossible if it returned the same value in both processes? A shell has a built-in cd command instead of running a separate cd program. Using what you saw about children and inheritance, explain why a separate program could not work. Which process states from the state diagram did your children pass through while they were sleeping? When were they running on the CPU?","Lab"],
  ["labs/01-processes.html#extension","Processes: launching, waiting and forking","Extension","Write a tiny shell: loop forever, read a line, split it into words, run it with subprocess.run (or fork + execvp + waitpid on Linux), and print the exit code. Then add a built-in cd and exit. You have just built the core of every command-line shell.","Lab"],
  ["labs/02-threads.html","Threads, race conditions and the GIL","Threads, race conditions and the GIL","Create threads, make them share a counter, watch updates disappear, and fix the problem with a lock. Then measure when threads actually make Python faster, and when you need separate processes instead. About 45 minutesAny systemRelated: Module 5 Threads, Module 6 Synchronization","Lab"],
  ["labs/02-threads.html#what-you-will-practice","Threads, race conditions and the GIL","What you will practice","Creating, starting and joining threads. Causing and observing a race condition. Protecting a critical section with a lock (mutual exclusion). Explaining why threads do not speed up CPU-bound Python code but processes do (Python's Global Interpreter Lock).","Lab"],
  ["labs/02-threads.html#part-a-a-race-condition","Threads, race conditions and the GIL","Part A: a race condition","Predict first: four threads each add 1 to a shared counter 2,000 times. What should the final value be? Without a lock, do you expect exactly that, more, or less? In C the race needs no help. Python's interpreter lock keeps most threads from running at the same moment, so the Python version calls time.sleep(0) to force a switch, which is a little contrived. In C the four threads really run on different CPU cores at once, so updates are lost on their own, much as they are in real programs. volatile stops the compiler from keeping the counter in a register, so the read and the write really happen. Build it with gcc -std=c99 -D_POSIX_C_SOURCE=200809L -pthread -O0 -o lab2_threads lab2_threads.c. C is so much faster that each thread adds 200,000 times, so the numbers differ from the Python output, but the lesson is the same. The time.sleep(0) in (2) is a teaching device. Because only one thread runs Python bytecode at a time, small programs often get lucky without it, which is dangerous: the bug is still there and shows up later under load. The C tab shows the race happening naturally, with no help. Run the Python version with python lab2_threads.py, or build the C version shown in its tab. Run either several times: the unsafe result changes every run. Example output Your “without a lock” number will be different every time. The “with a lock” line will always be correct. What to notice (1)–(3) counter += 1 is really three steps: read, add, write (the same load, add, store you stepped through in the race-condition lab). Here they are spelled out as separate lines. (2) time.sleep(0) makes the thread give up the CPU between the read and the write. That makes the race easy to see. Without it, Python's interpreter lock hides many races on small programs, which is dangerous because the bug is still there and shows up later under load. (3) Two threads that read the same old value both write back the same new value, so one increment is lost. Many updates are lost because each thread usually reads a value that another thread has already made stale. (4) The with lock: block makes read, add and write behave as one indivisible step. Only one thread can be inside at a time. (5) and (6) start() lets the threads run; join() waits for each one. Forgetting join() would print the counter before the threads finish.","Lab"],
  ["labs/02-threads.html#part-b-threads-versus-processes-the-gil","Threads, race conditions and the GIL","Part B: threads versus processes (the GIL)","CPython (the standard Python) has a Global Interpreter Lock: only one thread can run Python bytecode at a time. Predict first: will four threads finish four CPU-heavy tasks faster than doing them one after another? Will four processes? Run it with python lab2_gil.py. The tasks are pure calculation with no waiting. Example output From a 16-thread Windows PC. Your times will differ, but the pattern should match. What to notice (1) and (2) The task is pure calculation, and (2) is the baseline of running it four times in a row. (3) Threads gave almost no speedup for CPU-bound work, because the interpreter lock lets only one thread compute at a time. (4) Processes were faster (about 2.6× here). Each has its own interpreter and its own lock, so they truly run on different cores. They did not reach 4× because starting processes costs time and the work is split across only four tasks: compare this with Amdahl's law, where the serial part (here, starting up) limits the speedup. Processes do not share memory. Arguments and results are copied between them, which costs time and means they cannot simply update a shared variable. That is the trade-off for their isolation (see Module 5). The if __name__ == \"__main__\": guard is required on Windows and macOS because new processes re-import the file. Newer Python: Python 3.13 and later offer an experimental “free-threaded” build without the interpreter lock, and the results of Part B can differ there. The ideas, though (shared memory needs locks; processes isolate), apply to every language.","Lab"],
  ["labs/02-threads.html#try-it","Threads, race conditions and the GIL","Try it","Change THREADS to 1. Is the unsafe version still wrong? Why? Remove time.sleep(0) from the unsafe worker and run it a few times. Is it still wrong? What does that tell you about testing for race conditions? Move the with lock: so that it covers only the counter = temp + 1 line. Is it correct now? (Hint: think about which steps must be indivisible.) In Part B replace the work function with time.sleep(0.5) (waiting, not computing). Now do threads help? What kind of program benefits from threads in Python? Change TASKS in Part B to 8, then to 2. Predict the process time each time.","Lab"],
  ["labs/02-threads.html#common-mistakes","Threads, race conditions and the GIL","Common mistakes","Trusting a test that passed once. Races are timing-dependent. A passing run proves very little. Locking too little. The lock must cover the whole read-modify-write, not just the write. Locking too much. Holding a lock during slow work (disk, network) makes every other thread wait. Forgetting join(). The main program may print or exit before the work is done. Using threads for CPU-bound work in CPython and expecting a speedup.","Lab"],
  ["labs/02-threads.html#reflection-questions-good-journal-entries","Threads, race conditions and the GIL","Reflection questions (good journal entries)","Explain the unsafe result in your own words, using the three steps read, add and write. Why was the result so much smaller than 8000, not just a little smaller? A locked counter is correct but each increment now waits for the lock. Using ideas from Amdahl's law, explain how heavy locking can erase the benefit of adding more threads. When would you choose threads, and when processes, for a real program? Give one example of each.","Lab"],
  ["labs/02-threads.html#extension","Threads, race conditions and the GIL","Extension","Replace the shared counter with a queue.Queue: worker threads put 1 on the queue and one collector thread adds them up. Why is there now no need for a lock in your own code? (The queue does the locking for you.) Then compare the speed of the lock version and the queue version.","Lab"],
  ["labs/03-resources.html","Exploring system resources with Python","Exploring system resources with Python","Ask the operating system what it knows about your computer: CPU use, memory, swap, disk space and the processes that are running. Then explain what each number means in terms of the concepts from the lessons. About 40 minutesAny systemNeeds the psutil packageRelated: Module 4, Module 8, Module 9, Module 10","Lab"],
  ["labs/03-resources.html#what-you-will-practice","Exploring system resources with Python","What you will practice","Using the standard library (os, platform, shutil) for basic system facts. Using psutil to read CPU, memory, swap and process information. Telling apart free and available memory, logical and physical cores, and resident and virtual memory. Handling processes you are not allowed to inspect, or that disappear while you look.","Lab"],
  ["labs/03-resources.html#setup","Exploring system resources with Python","Setup","On Windows you can use py -m pip install psutil. On Codio it may already be installed. If it is missing the script still prints the standard-library facts and tells you what to install.","Lab"],
  ["labs/03-resources.html#the-script","Exploring system resources with Python","The script","Predict first: before running it, estimate how much RAM your computer has, how much is in use, and which program uses the most memory. Then compare. Run it with python lab3_resources.py. Example output An illustrative example from a Linux laptop. Your numbers and process names will be different. What to notice Logical versus physical cores. A hardware thread (hyper-threading) lets one physical core look like two logical CPUs to the scheduler. The scheduler in Module 4 schedules onto logical CPUs. (1) cpu_percent(interval=1) measures busy time over a period. Calling it without an interval returns a meaningless first value because there is nothing to compare against. Available is not free. Free memory is memory nobody is using at all. Available also counts memory the OS can take back instantly, such as disk cache. A healthy system often has almost no free memory: the OS uses spare RAM as a cache (see Module 9). Swap is the backing store for virtual memory. Heavy swap use together with a busy disk is a sign of thrashing. (2) process_iter with a list of attributes fetches them efficiently, and it returns None for any value the OS refuses to show. Checking for None prevents crashes on protected processes. RSS (resident set size) is the memory a process currently has in RAM. It is not the same as its virtual size, which includes memory that is mapped but not loaded. (3) and (4) psutil.Process() with no argument means “this process”, and parents() walks up the process tree you built in Lab 1, all the way to init/systemd or the system's first process. Which operating-system concept is behind each number? You read…It reflects…Where it is taught CPU % per coreHow busy the scheduler keeps each CPUModule 4 Threads per processConcurrency inside one address spaceModule 5 RAM total, available, RSSPhysical memory management and the page cacheModule 8 Swap in usePages evicted by page replacementModule 9 Disk usedFile-system capacity and allocationModule 11 Process status (running, sleeping, zombie)The process state diagramModule 3","Lab"],
  ["labs/03-resources.html#try-it","Exploring system resources with Python","Try it","Run the script, then open a few big programs (a browser with many tabs) and run it again. Which numbers changed the most? Add a sample of CPU use for the whole system every second for ten seconds. Start something heavy halfway through and watch the numbers. List the five processes with the most threads. Is it the same list as the top memory users? Print how many processes have each status (running, sleeping, and so on) using collections.Counter. Do you ever see a zombie? Where would you expect one (see Lab 1)? Use psutil.disk_io_counters() or psutil.net_io_counters() before and after downloading a large file. What is the difference?","Lab"],
  ["labs/03-resources.html#common-mistakes","Exploring system resources with Python","Common mistakes","Reading cpu_percent() once with no interval. The first call is not meaningful. Treating “free” as “available”. A system with 200 MB free may have gigabytes available. Not handling AccessDenied and NoSuchProcess. Processes can vanish between listing them and reading their details, and some are protected. Assuming every field exists on every OS. Some values (such as load average) are missing or different on Windows or macOS.","Lab"],
  ["labs/03-resources.html#reflection-questions-good-journal-entries","Exploring system resources with Python","Reflection questions (good journal entries)","Your computer shows 95% of RAM “in use” but runs smoothly. Using the idea of the page cache, explain how that can be normal. What numbers would make you worried instead? Pick the process using the most memory. Explain, using RSS and virtual memory, what that number does and does not tell you. Which resource (CPU, memory, disk) was the busiest on your machine, and what evidence from the script shows it?","Lab"],
  ["labs/03-resources.html#extension","Exploring system resources with Python","Extension","Build a small top-style monitor: clear the screen every two seconds and show CPU %, memory %, and the five busiest processes by CPU. (Hint: call proc.cpu_percent(None) once for all processes to prime it, sleep, then read it again.) Save each sample to a CSV file and chart it afterward.","Lab"],
  ["labs/04-semaphores-deadlock.html","Semaphores and deadlock prevention","Semaphores and deadlock prevention","Coordinate producers and consumers with semaphores so a bounded buffer never overflows or underflows. Then build the classic dining-philosophers deadlock, detect it, and prevent it by breaking one of the four deadlock conditions. About 50 minutesAny systemRelated: Module 6 Synchronization, Module 7 Deadlocks","Lab"],
  ["labs/04-semaphores-deadlock.html#what-you-will-practice","Semaphores and deadlock prevention","What you will practice","Using semaphores as counters of free and filled slots. Combining a semaphore-based protocol with a mutex that protects the buffer. Creating a deadlock on purpose and recognizing its symptoms. Preventing it by removing circular wait with a resource ordering.","Lab"],
  ["labs/04-semaphores-deadlock.html#the-script","Semaphores and deadlock prevention","The script","One script has two parts: the bounded buffer (producers and consumers), then the dining philosophers. Predict first: in Part B all five philosophers pick up their left fork first. What do you expect to happen? Python's threading.Semaphore, threading.Barrier and acquire(timeout=...) are built in, which is why this version is much shorter. Use it to focus on the protocol: which semaphore to wait on, in what order. C needs more code, and the extra code is the lesson. There is no counting semaphore that works everywhere (POSIX unnamed semaphores are missing on macOS), so this version builds one from a mutex and a condition variable. That is how semaphores are actually implemented: semaphore_wait sleeps on the condition variable until the count is positive, and semaphore_signal increments it and wakes a sleeper. try_lock_for polls because there is no portable timed lock. Build it with gcc -std=c99 -D_POSIX_C_SOURCE=200809L -pthread -o lab4_sync lab4_sync.c. It prints the same lines as the Python version (the bounded buffer's “largest size seen” can be 1, 2 or 3). Run it with python lab4_sync.py (or the C build in its tab). It takes about three seconds, mostly spent waiting for the deadlock to be declared. Example output","Lab"],
  ["labs/04-semaphores-deadlock.html#part-a-what-to-notice-bounded-buffer","Semaphores and deadlock prevention","Part A: what to notice (bounded buffer)","(1) empty counts free slots and starts at the buffer size. A producer must acquire (wait on) it before adding an item, so it blocks when the buffer is full. (2) full counts filled slots and starts at 0. A consumer must acquire it before removing an item, so it blocks when the buffer is empty. (3) mutex is a separate lock that protects the deque itself. The semaphores decide when a thread may proceed; the mutex makes the actual insert or remove safe. These are exactly the empty = N, full = 0, mutex = 1 initial values from the lesson. acquire() is wait() (P) and release() is signal() (V). The “largest buffer size seen” can never exceed the limit. That is the guarantee the empty semaphore provides.","Lab"],
  ["labs/04-semaphores-deadlock.html#part-b-what-to-notice-dining-philosophers","Semaphores and deadlock prevention","Part B: what to notice (dining philosophers)","(4) Holding one fork while waiting for the second (hold and wait) is what makes deadlock possible. The short sleep makes sure every philosopher is holding a fork before anyone tries for the second one, so the deadlock happens every time. All four deadlock conditions are present in the naive version: forks are exclusive (mutual exclusion), each philosopher holds one and waits for another (hold and wait), forks cannot be taken away (no preemption), and philosopher 0 waits for 1 waits for 2 … waits for 4 waits for 0 (circular wait). See the four conditions. (5) Detecting the deadlock. The program cannot know a deadlock exists. It only sees that nobody has finished after two seconds. Real systems use timeouts and watchdogs the same way, then recover (here by sounding an alarm so the threads give up). The fix: if every philosopher picks up the lower-numbered fork first, philosopher 4 reaches for fork 0 before fork 4. A cycle of waiting can no longer form (circular wait is impossible), so at least one philosopher can always eat. Try the dining-philosophers simulator to see the same two strategies.","Lab"],
  ["labs/04-semaphores-deadlock.html#try-it","Semaphores and deadlock prevention","Try it","Make the bounded buffer deadlock. In producer, swap the order so it takes mutex before empty.acquire(). Run it with a small buffer. What happens, and why? (The lesson warns about exactly this.) Try a different fix. Add a third strategy: a Semaphore(N - 1) “waiter” that only lets four philosophers sit at once. Which deadlock condition does that break? Change PATIENCE to 0.5. Does the naive version still report a deadlock? What if it is too short for the ordered version to finish? Change BUFFER_SIZE to 1 and then 10. Does the “largest size seen” follow it? Give each philosopher a random thinking time with random.uniform. Is the naive version still deadlocked every time? What does that tell you about intermittent deadlocks in real programs?","Lab"],
  ["labs/04-semaphores-deadlock.html#common-mistakes","Semaphores and deadlock prevention","Common mistakes","Acquiring in the wrong order. Taking the mutex before the semaphore can block everyone while holding the lock. Forgetting to release (especially when an exception happens). Use with lock: so release is automatic. Releasing a semaphore too many times silently raises its count. threading.BoundedSemaphore raises an error instead, which helps catch the bug. Joining threads without a timeout. A deadlocked program just hangs and gives you no clue. Timeouts turn a hang into a message.","Lab"],
  ["labs/04-semaphores-deadlock.html#reflection-questions-good-journal-entries","Semaphores and deadlock prevention","Reflection questions (good journal entries)","Why are two kinds of synchronization (semaphores and a mutex) needed in the bounded buffer? What could go wrong with only the semaphores? The deadlock was easy to cause here and might be rare in a real program. Why do intermittent deadlocks make better evidence of a design flaw than a one-off hang? Compare prevention (ordering the forks) with avoidance (the Banker's algorithm). Which would you choose for this problem and why?","Lab"],
  ["labs/04-semaphores-deadlock.html#extension","Semaphores and deadlock prevention","Extension","Implement the readers–writers problem: many reader threads may read a shared value at once, but a writer needs exclusive access. First make it work, then add a “writer waiting” rule so readers cannot starve the writer. What did you have to change?","Lab"]
];
